window.REALTEST_DATA = {"id":8,"title":"Time Mode 3 · Dojo bổ sung","provider":"Tutorials Dojo","source":"DOJOS/Sections/TIME_MODE/tf3/📊 Your Progress - Tutorials Dojo.html","minutes":70,"supplementary":true,"questions":[{"id":1,"sourceNumber":1,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A company operates with two AWS accounts - one for Production (Prod) and another for Security. The company stores data in various S3 buckets across multiple AWS regions. They are enforcing a strict policy requiring that any action taken on an S3 bucket in the Prod account be logged in the Security account.</p>\n<p>Which of the following would meet the requirements?</p>","answers":["Create a multi-region trail in AWS CloudTrail in the Prod account and direct the logs to an S3 bucket in the Security account.","Use AWS Config in the Prod account and send notifications of changes to an SNS topic in the Security account. Create an aggregator to collect config data from multiple regions.","Enable versioning on the S3 bucket in the Prod account and replicate the versions to an S3 bucket in the Security account.","Use AWS Lambda in the Prod account to copy any new objects in the S3 bucket to an S3 bucket in the Security account."],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>You can have CloudTrail deliver log files from multiple AWS accounts into a single Amazon S3 bucket. For example, you have four AWS accounts with account IDs 111111111111, 222222222222, 333333333333, and 444444444444, and you can configure CloudTrail to deliver log files from all four of these accounts to a bucket belonging to account 111111111111.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/a5d7b64f003eb512b03cd28fc4fcd2e518493bc4eca36e6bd98f4d0566dd68a8.png\"></p>\n<p>AWS CloudTrail records actions taken by a user, role, or an AWS service in AWS. If a bucket is designated as a CloudTrail log bucket, CloudTrail logs API calls made on this bucket. Creating a multi-region trail in the Prod account and directing the logs to an S3 bucket in the Security account ensures that every action taken on S3 buckets, regardless of the region, is captured and stored securely in a centralized location.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Create a multi-region trail in AWS CloudTrail in the Prod account and direct the logs to an S3 bucket in the Security account.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <strong><span>Use AWS Config in the Prod account and send notifications of changes to an SNS topic in the Security account. Create an aggregator to collect config data from multiple regions</span></strong> is incorrect. While AWS Config can track changes in configurations and resource states over time, it is not designed specifically for logging every action taken on S3 buckets (such as data access or modification actions). Thus, it may not capture all types of actions as required by the policy.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <strong><span>Enable versioning on the S3 bucket in the Prod account and replicate the versions to an S3 bucket in the Security account</span></strong> is incorrect. This option will simply help in maintaining a backup of the data and protecting against accidental deletions or overwrites. It does not log user actions or API calls as required by the company's policy.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <strong><span>Use AWS Lambda in the Prod account to copy any new objects in the S3 bucket to an S3 bucket in the Security account</span></strong> is incorrect because this would only replicate the data after actions have been taken, without providing any information regarding the details of the actions themselves, such as who accessed the data or when it was accessed.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-receive-logs-from-multiple-accounts.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-receive-logs-from-multiple-accounts.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/sharing-aws-cloudtrail-log-files-between-accounts/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/sharing-aws-cloudtrail-log-files-between-accounts/</a></p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS CloudTrail Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-cloudtrail/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-cloudtrail/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":2,"sourceNumber":2,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A data engineer has created an AWS Lambda function that will read and write data to an Amazon RDS for MySQL database. The database is located in a private subnet of a VPC. The Lambda function’s execution role has already been granted the <code>AWSLambdaVPCAccessExecutionRole</code> managed policy.</p>\n<p>What set of steps must be done next to enable database access for the function? (Select TWO.)</p>","answers":["Grant the Lambda function’s execution role with full RDS permissions.","Connect the Lambda function to the same VPC as the database.","Create a new VPC and connect it to the Lambda function. Enable VPC peering with the database’s VPC.","Grant the Lambda function's execution role read and write permissions for RDS.","Attach the same security group to the Lambda function and RDS database, allowing inbound and outbound traffic on port 3306."],"correct":[1,4],"explanation":"<p>AWS Lambda functions do not reside within a Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) by default. They are executed in a managed environment by AWS, which is outside of your VPC. This setup means that if your Lambda function needs to interact with resources located in a private subnet of a VPC, such as an Amazon RDS for MySQL database, special configurations are required to enable this connectivity.</p>\n<p>When connecting a Lambda function to private resources like a database in a private subnet, the first step is to configure the function to access the VPC. This involves specifying the VPC ID, subnet IDs, and security group IDs that the Lambda function will use.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/21507ad33427b4e92204201902779ab860cc21c091ac5dc37fc3f5df63eedf27.jpg\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>To perform this configuration:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Connect the Lambda Function to the VPC:</strong> This is done by selecting the VPC and the specific private subnets within the VPC where the database resides. This step ensures that the Lambda function can operate within the network that houses the database.</li>\n<li><strong>Security Group Configuration:</strong> Attach a security group to the Lambda function that allows it to communicate with the RDS instance. Typically, this involves allowing inbound and outbound traffic on port 3306 (the default port for MySQL databases) between the Lambda function and the RDS database.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>An important part of this configuration involves assigning the correct permissions to the Lambda function's execution role. AWS provides the <code>AWSLambdaVPCAccessExecutionRole</code> managed policy, which grants the Lambda function the necessary permissions to create, configure, and manage network interfaces within the VPC. This managed policy includes permissions for actions like <code>ec2:CreateNetworkInterface</code>, <code>ec2:DescribeNetworkInterfaces</code>, <code>ec2:DeleteNetworkInterface</code>, etc., which are essential for the Lambda function to interact with VPC resources.</p>\n<p>In the scenario, you can deploy a Lambda function in the same VPC and use the same self-referencing security group for both RDS and Lambda. For the RDS instance, an inbound rule allowing traffic on port 3306 is necessary to accept traffic from the Lambda function. On the other hand, configuring the security group to allow outbound traffic on port 3306 allows the Lambda function to make outbound calls to the RDS database.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answers are:</p>\n<p><span>- <strong>Connect the Lambda function to the same VPC as the database.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span>- <strong>Attach the same security group to the Lambda function and RDS database, allowing inbound and outbound traffic on port 3306.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Grant the Lambda function’s execution role with full RDS permissions </strong></span>is incorrect. Merely granting IAM permissions does not address the network connectivity requirement between the Lambda function and the RDS database in a private subnet.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <strong><span>Create a new VPC and connect it to the Lambda function. Enable VPC peering with the database’s VPC </span></strong>is incorrect. This approach introduces unnecessary complexity and is not required for the Lambda function to access the RDS database within the same VPC.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <strong><span>Grant the Lambda function's execution role read and write permissions for RDS </span></strong> is incorrect. Just like the other incorrect option, IAM permissions related to RDS generally pertain to management operations, such as creating and deleting an RDS instance, not to how the Lambda function writes and reads data to the actual database. These permissions do not affect the Lambda function's ability to interact with the data within the RDS database.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/connect-lambda-to-an-rds-instance\">https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/connect-lambda-to-an-rds-instance</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/configuration-vpc.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/configuration-vpc.html</a></p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Lambda Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":3,"sourceNumber":3,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A multinational corporation has customer data from various countries stored in an Amazon S3 bucket. The data analysts in the team are assigned to specific countries and should only have access to the customer data from their respective countries. The corporation wants to enforce strict access policies to ensure data privacy and compliance.</p>\n<p>Which of these options would most efficiently and securely manage and control access to customer data while ensuring compliance with the corporation’s policies?</p>","answers":["Incorporate the S3 bucket as a data lake location within AWS Lake Formation. Utilize the granular security features of Lake Formation, such as row-level security, to enforce the corporation’s access policies effectively.","Establish individual tables for each country’s customer data within the Amazon S3 bucket. Grant access to each data analyst specifically based on the country they are responsible for.","Use AWS Glue to catalog the data in the S3 bucket and set up separate databases for each country. Provide access to each analyst based on the country that the analyst serves.","Import the customer data into Amazon Redshift. Construct a distinct view for each country’s data. Generate separate IAM roles for each country to provide precise access to the respective country’s data. Assign the corresponding roles to the analysts based on their country of service."],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p><strong>AWS Lake Formation</strong> is a fully managed service that helps you build, secure, and manage data lakes, and provide access control for data in the data lake. AWS Lake Formation row-level permissions allow you to provide access to specific rows in a table based on data compliance and governance policies. If you have large tables storing billions of records, you need a way to enable different users and teams to access only the data they are allowed to see. Row-level access control is a simple and performant way to protect data while giving users access to the data they need to perform their job. Lake Formation provides centralized auditing and compliance reporting by identifying which principals accessed what data, when, and through which services.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/7d9be3a7c775a068740a9aa0c9d13811c6cd1ef49e4ca3bb4d6a533cddfe9a53.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>If you have large fact tables storing billions of records, you need a way to enable different users and teams to access only the data they’re allowed to see. Row-level access control is a simple and performant way to protect data while giving users access to the data they need to perform their jobs.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Incorporate the S3 bucket as a data lake location within AWS Lake Formation. Utilize the granular security features of Lake Formation, such as row-level security, to enforce the corporation’s access policies effectively.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Establish individual tables for each country’s customer data within the Amazon S3 bucket. Grant access to each data analyst specifically based on the country they are responsible for</strong></span> is incorrect because it only provides a rudimentary level of access control. While creating separate tables for each country’s data might seem like a good idea, it doesn’t provide the necessary granularity for access control. AWS recommends using IAM policies for access control. Sharing a table with multiple users can lead to data leaks if not managed properly.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use AWS Glue to catalog the data in the S3 bucket and set up separate databases for each country. Provide access to each analyst based on the country that the analyst serves </strong></span>is incorrect because it primarily focuses on the physical location of the data, which does not directly relate to access control or compliance with the corporation’s policies. While storing data in AWS Regions close to the customers can improve data access speed and reduce latency, it doesn’t help with access control.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Import the customer data into Amazon Redshift. Construct a distinct view for each country’s data. Generate separate IAM roles for each country to provide precise access to the respective country’s data. Assign the corresponding roles to the analysts based on their country of service</strong></span> is incorrect because it simply moves the data to a different service (Amazon Redshift) without providing a comprehensive solution for enforcing access policies at a granular level. While Amazon Redshift allows for the creation of views and the assignment of IAM roles, it doesn’t provide the same level of fine-grained access control as AWS Lake Formation.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/cbac-tutorial.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/cbac-tutorial.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/part-2-effective-data-lakes-using-aws-lake-formation-secure-data-lakes-with-row-level-access-control/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/part-2-effective-data-lakes-using-aws-lake-formation-secure-data-lakes-with-row-level-access-control/</a></p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Lake Formation Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":4,"sourceNumber":4,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p><span>A company ingests hundreds of unstructured files in Amazon S3 every day. The data must be cataloged using an AWS Glue crawler, and the results must be output to an Glue Data Catalog database.</span></p>\n<p><span>Which of the following would meet this requirement in the LEAST amount of effort?</span></p>","answers":["<span>Create an IAM service role with the <code>AWSGlueServiceRole</code> managed policy and attach this role to the crawler. Set up the Glue Crawler to run daily and specify the database in the Glue Data Catalog.</span>","<span>Create an IAM service role with the <code>AmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess</code> policy and attach this role to the crawler. Set up the Glue Crawler to run daily and specify a different database. Run the crawler to catalog the data.</span>","<span>Create an IAM service role with the <code>AWSGlueServiceRole</code> policy and attach this role to the crawler. Set up the Glue Crawler to run on-demand and specify the database in the Glue Data Catalog.</span>","<span>Create an IAM service role with the <code>AmazonS3FullAccess</code> policy and attach this role to the crawler. Set up the Glue Crawler to run daily and specify a new S3 path as the target location. Run the crawler to catalog the data.</span>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>AWS Glue is a fully managed extract, transform, and load (ETL) service that makes it easy to prepare and load your data for analytics. An AWS Glue crawler is a component of AWS Glue that automates the process of discovering, classifying, and cataloging data stored in various data stores. The crawler scans your data sources, identifies data formats, infers schemas, and then creates or updates metadata tables in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. This catalog acts as a central metadata repository that AWS Glue and other Amazon services use for ETL, data querying, and data discovery tasks.</p>\n<p>To perform its operations, a crawler needs to interact with other AWS services on your behalf. This is achieved by using an IAM role that has the necessary permissions. When you create an IAM role for AWS Glue, you grant your IAM role permissions that AWS Glue can assume when calling other services on your behalf. This includes access to Amazon S3 for any sources, targets, scripts, and temporary directories that you use with AWS Glue.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/76d3c851606f5602efae4982d0301c961825b8c19d655013b00f3378b68fcfcf.png\" alt=\"AWSGlueServiceRole\"></p>\n<p>The&nbsp;<code>AWSGlueServiceRole</code>&nbsp;managed policy provides the necessary permissions for AWS Glue crawler to access the resources it needs for reading data from Amazon S3 and writing metadata to AWS Glue Data Catalog.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/8f5d46649e1fd1d7b236832cb6ae02d3c00703cadd447658e122661e44ad67bb.png\" alt=\"Crawler Sschedule\"></p>\n<p>Finally, you configure the AWS Glue Crawler to run daily and write the output to a specified database in AWS Glue Data Catalog. This meets the requirement of cataloging the data every day.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Create an IAM service role with the <code>AWSGlueServiceRole</code> managed policy and attach this role to the crawler. Set up the Glue Crawler to run daily and specify the database in the Glue Data Catalog.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Create an IAM service role with the <code>AmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess</code> policy and attach this role to the crawler. Set up the Glue Crawler to run daily and specify a different database. Run the crawler to catalog the data</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. The&nbsp;<code>AmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess</code>&nbsp;policy only provides read access to S3 resources and is not sufficient for&nbsp;the crawler's tasks, such as writing metadata to the AWS Glue Data Catalog.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Create an IAM service role with the <code>AWSGlueServiceRole</code> policy and attach this role to the crawler. Set up the Glue Crawler to run on-demand and specify the database in the Glue Data Catalog</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect.&nbsp;Setting up the crawler to run on-demand instead of on a regular schedule typically requires manual intervention each time the data needs to be cataloged. This approach increases operational effort, especially given the daily ingestion of hundreds of files.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Create an IAM service role with the <code>AmazonS3FullAccess</code> policy and attach this role to the crawler. Set up the Glue Crawler to run daily and specify a new S3 path as the target location. Run the crawler to catalog the data&nbsp;</strong></span>is incorrect. This policy grants broader S3 permissions than necessary for the crawler's purpose. Similar to the other incorrect option, this policy does not contain the relevant Glue permissions for the crawler. Also, the main task of a Glue crawler is to catalog data into AWS Glue Data Catalog.&nbsp; The target for a crawler's output should be a Data Catalog database where the table definitions are stored, not another S3 path location.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/create-an-iam-role.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/create-an-iam-role.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/set-up-iam.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/set-up-iam.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this&nbsp;AWS Identity and Access Management Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-identity-and-access-management-iam/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-identity-and-access-management-iam/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":5,"sourceNumber":5,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A company has a production AWS account and a separate audit AWS account. The company’s policy requires all logs from the production account to be sent to the audit account for real-time analysis and long-term storage.</p>\n<p>The production account uses Amazon CloudWatch Logs to aggregate logs from Amazon EC2 instances. A data engineer must set up a system to stream these logs to an Amazon Kinesis Data Stream in the audit account.</p>\n<p>Which of the following options would meet the company’s requirements?</p>","answers":["Set up a target data stream within the audit AWS account. Create an IAM role and a trust policy to authorize CloudWatch Logs to send data into the stream. Establish a subscription filter in the production AWS account.","Set up a target data stream within the production AWS account. Within the production AWS account, create an IAM role with cross-account permissions for Kinesis Data Streams located in the audit AWS account.","Set up a target data stream within the audit AWS account. Create an IAM role and a trust policy to authorize CloudWatch Logs to send data into the stream. Establish a subscription filter in the audit AWS account.","Utilize AWS CloudTrail within the audit AWS account to directly access and analyze the logs located in the production AWS account."],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>You can use subscriptions to get access to a real-time feed of log events from CloudWatch Logs and have it delivered to other services such as an Amazon Kinesis stream, an Amazon Data Firehose stream, or AWS Lambda for custom processing, analysis, or loading to other systems. When log events are sent to the receiving service, they are base64 encoded and compressed with the gzip format.</p>\n<p>To begin subscribing to log events, create the receiving resource, such as a Kinesis Data Streams stream, where the events will be delivered. A subscription filter defines the filter pattern to use for filtering which log events get delivered to your AWS resource, as well as information about where to send matching log events.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/468e5eb4de352a0b5e0f48d099db76f6e638b2d296e55c96058fdb1e04793f85.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>You can create subscriptions at the account level and at the log group level. Each account can have one account-level subscription filter. Each log group can have up to two subscription filters associated with it.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:&nbsp;<span><strong>Set up a target data stream within the audit AWS account. Create an IAM role and a trust policy to authorize CloudWatch Logs to send data into the stream. Establish a subscription filter in the production AWS account.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Set up a target data stream within the production AWS account. Within the production AWS account, create an IAM role with cross-account permissions for Kinesis Data Streams located in the audit AWS account</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect.&nbsp;The logs need to be sent to the audit AWS account, not stored in the production account. Additionally, while it does create an IAM role with cross-account permissions, it does so in the wrong account. The IAM role and trust policy need to be created in the audit AWS account to allow CloudWatch Logs to put data into the stream</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Set up a target data stream within the audit AWS account. Create an IAM role and a trust policy to authorize CloudWatch Logs to send data into the stream. Establish a subscription filter in the audit AWS account</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because it sets up the subscription filter in the audit account instead of the production account. The subscription filter needs to be in the production AWS account to capture the logs and send them to the audit account. While it correctly sets up a data stream and IAM role in the audit AWS account, the misplacement of the subscription filter makes this option incorrect.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Utilize AWS CloudTrail within the audit AWS account to directly access and analyze the logs located in the production AWS account</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect.&nbsp;AWS CloudTrail can track user activity and API usage, but it's not capable of streaming logs between accounts. Therefore, this option does not meet the company’s requirements.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/Subscriptions.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/Subscriptions.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/SubscriptionFilters.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/SubscriptionFilters.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon CloudWatch Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-cloudwatch/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-cloudwatch/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":6,"sourceNumber":6,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A developer wants to utilize AWS Lambda to manipulate data in their existing RDS database instance. The database instance is in a private subnet with the PosgestSQL database engine. The developer must connect to the database at the default port (5432) and run queries from the lambda without traversing the public internet.</p>\n<p>Which solution will meet the requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?</p>","answers":["Deploy the Lambda in the same private subnet. Allow traffic from Lambda's security group through the 5432 port in the RDS database instance's security group.","Migrate the RDS database instance to a public subnet. Configure the Lambda to connect to RDS with credentials stored in Secrets Manager.","Enable IAM DB Authentication in the DB instance. Create an IAM role that allows Amazon RDS access and attach it to the Lambda.","Deploy the Lambda in a different VPC. Allow traffic from Lambda's security group through the 5432 port in the RDS database instance's security group."],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>You can use the <strong>Amazon RDS</strong> console to simplify setting up a connection between a Lambda function and a DB instance. Often, your DB instance is in a private subnet within a VPC. The Lambda function can be used by applications to access your private DB instance.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/1d0305108960bc3f83dee9a5fe24466c0bec6ec7f3d62fb0fd68aceeb585334d.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>When connecting a Lambda function to an RDS instance in the same VPC, use the following networking configurations.</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: By default, all subnets within a VPC contain a local route. The destination is the VPC's Classless Inter-Domain Routing (CIDR), and the target is local.</p>\n<p>For <strong>Security Groups</strong>:</p>\n<p>For instances that are attached to the same security group, make the security group the source for the inbound rule. Make the security group the destination for the outbound rule.</p>\n<p>For example, if the Lambda function and RDS instance are both in security group sg-abcd1234, then each instance has the following inbound and outbound rules.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/6c26e55ed1fea8b6d1758a726f635b4fcafbdb59735daeb82af63c2a53dc06fe.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>For instance, in different security groups, make sure that both security groups allow access to each other.</p>\n<p>For example, if the Lambda function is in security group sg-1234 and the RDS instance is in sg-abcd, then each group has the following rules:</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/bd2e47adec27f5c3c94e9c40dddeb511ee70beee8e5a72896c0cd78d894f88be.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>Since you're gonna use the PostgreSQL engine, use the 5432 port in your security group configuration.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Deploy the Lambda in the same private subnet. Allow traffic from Lambda's security group through the 5432 port in the RDS database instance's security group.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Migrate the RDS Database instance to a public subnet. Configure the Lambda to connect to RDS with credentials stored in Secrets Manager</strong></span> is incorrect. Although this solution will allow Lambda to connect, it is unrecommended to deploy the database instance in a public subnet. It will open the database to unwanted connections. The Lambda will connect to the database through the internet, which is undesirable. For security reasons, this solution is incorrect.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Enable IAM DB Authentication in the DB instance. Create an IAM role that allows Amazon RDS access and attach it to the Lambda</strong></span> is incorrect. While this solution can connect the Lambda to the RDS database instance, it still cannot satisfy the requirement not to use the internet. You can deploy the Lambda in the same private subnet and use IAM db authentication to connect to the database without a password. However, to use the IAM authentication, you must ensure that the Lambda can connect to the internet. Hence, the subnet route table needs to include a NAT gateway. The traffic will have to traverse the internet, which is undesirable.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Deploy the Lambda in a different VPC.&nbsp;Allow traffic from Lambda's security group through the 5432 port in the RDS database instance's security group</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. This option makes the network configuration more complicated without any clear benefits. Since it involves a different VPC, you would need to set up VPC peering or transit gateways to facilitate communication between the two VPCs.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/connect-lambda-to-an-rds-instance\">https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/connect-lambda-to-an-rds-instance</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/lambda-rds-connect.html#lambda-rds-connect-connecting\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/lambda-rds-connect.html#lambda-rds-connect-connecting</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/services-rds.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/services-rds.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Lambda Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":7,"sourceNumber":8,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A large e-commerce company has a data lake stored in Amazon S3, which contains a vast amount of customer transaction data in JSON format. The company wants to analyze this data to gain insights into customer behavior and improve its marketing strategies.</p>\n<p>Amazon Athena is chosen for querying the data due to its serverless nature. However, the data engineering team is having difficulties dealing with JSON data, especially when they need to pivot it for analysis.</p>\n<p>How should a solution be designed to enable efficient querying of the JSON data in the S3 data lake using Amazon Athena, considering the need to pivot the data?</p>","answers":["<span>Utilize the <code>JSON SerDe</code> library in Athena to parse the JSON data and create a table in Athena. Then, use SQL queries with <code>CASE</code> statements to pivot the data.</span>","<span>Load the JSON data from S3 into Amazon Redshift, then use the <code>PIVOT</code> function in Redshift to pivot the data.</span>","<span>Convert the JSON data into CSV format using AWS Glue, then create a table in Athena to query the data. Use SQL queries with <code>GROUP BY</code> and <code>CASE</code> statements to pivot the data.</span>","Use AWS Lambda to parse the JSON data and store it in DynamoDB. Then, use Athena to query the data in DynamoDB."],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Amazon Athena is a serverless interactive query service that makes it easy to analyze data in Amazon S3 using standard SQL. It’s designed to handle large-scale datasets and can execute queries in parallel, making it a powerful tool for data analysis. In the context of the question, the company is dealing with JSON data in an S3 data lake and wants to pivot this data for analysis. Athena uses libraries called SerDes (Serializer/Deserializer) to interpret the input data format. For JSON data, Athena uses the&nbsp;<code>org.openx.data.jsonserde.JsonSerDe</code>&nbsp;library, also known as&nbsp;<code>JSON SerDe</code>. This library allows Athena to interpret the JSON data, parse it into a structured format that can be queried using SQL.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/cda4017b02db9348cb0c201a8ac0ba477a093f8fed1e5c960cf8eb6abd6bc933.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>Once the JSON data is parsed using the&nbsp;<code>JSON SerDe</code>&nbsp;library, a table can be created in Athena. This table represents the structured format of the JSON data and can be queried directly using SQL. Pivoting data is a common operation in data analysis where you transform the data from a long format to a wide format. In SQL, this can be achieved using&nbsp;<code>CASE</code>&nbsp;statements. A&nbsp;<code>CASE</code>&nbsp;statement lets you create new columns based on conditions in the data. This way, you can pivot the data based on your needs. This approach leverages the serverless nature of Athena, which means you don’t have to worry about managing infrastructure or scaling resources.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:<span><strong>&nbsp;</strong><strong>Utilize the&nbsp;<code>JSON SerDe</code>&nbsp;library in Amazon Athena to parse the JSON data and create a table in Athena. Then, use SQL queries with&nbsp;<code>CASE</code>&nbsp;statements to pivot the data.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Load the JSON data from S3 into Amazon Redshift, then use the&nbsp;<code>PIVOT</code>&nbsp;function in Redshift to pivot the data</strong></span> is&nbsp;incorrect&nbsp;because while it’s possible to load data from S3 into Redshift and use the&nbsp;<code>PIVOT</code>&nbsp;function, this would involve additional data transfer and storage costs. Moreover, it doesn’t utilize the serverless nature of Athena which can directly query data in S3.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<strong><span>Convert the JSON data into CSV format using AWS Glue, then create a table in Athena to query the data. Use SQL queries with&nbsp;<code>GROUP BY</code>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<code>CASE</code>&nbsp;statements to pivot the data&nbsp;</span></strong>is&nbsp;incorrect&nbsp;because it involves an additional step of converting JSON data into CSV format using AWS Glue. This might not be necessary as Athena can directly query JSON data using the&nbsp;<code>JSON SerDe</code>&nbsp;library.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Use AWS Lambda to parse the JSON data and store it in DynamoDB. Then, use Athena to query the data in DynamoDB</strong></span>&nbsp;is&nbsp;incorrect. While this could work, loading the data in DynamoDB adds an unnecessary step in the workflow. Athena can directly query the data stored in Amazon S3 without any data movement involved.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/serde-about.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/serde-about.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/openx-json-serde.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/openx-json-serde.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Athena Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":8,"sourceNumber":17,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A data engineer is working on a project that involves processing and transforming large amounts of data. The data is stored in various formats and locations. The engineer needs a solution that allows for interactive development and debugging, supports real-time data processing, and doesn’t require provisioning or managing clusters. The solution should also integrate well with other AWS services.</p>\n<p>Which of the following AWS services would best meet the requirements?</p>","answers":["Amazon EMR","AWS Glue Data Catalog","AWS Glue Crawlers","AWS Glue Studio Job Notebooks"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p><strong>AWS Glue Studio Notebook</strong>&nbsp;provides an interactive development environment that allows data engineers to write, edit, and test their ETL scripts. The notebooks are based on Jupyter Notebooks, a popular open-source web application that allows you to create and share documents that contain live code, equations, visualizations, and narrative text. This makes it easier for data engineers to develop and debug their ETL scripts interactively, reducing the overall development timeline.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/72f01c7cb5d57fd4857e236ffcd38474893e528e72fcad4f9116cbbf674f8045.png\"></p>\n<p>AWS Glue Studio Notebook also support real-time data processing. This is particularly useful when data is sourced from IoT devices, change data capture (CDC) services, and streaming services such as Amazon Kinesis and Apache Kafka.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:&nbsp;<span><strong>AWS Glue Studio Job Notebooks.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Amazon EMR</strong></span> is incorrect because it typically requires cluster provisioning and management, which doesn't align with the engineer's requirement for a solution that doesn't require managing clusters.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>AWS Glue Data Catalog&nbsp;</strong></span>is incorrect because the AWS Glue Data Catalog is just a managed service that lets you store, annotate, and share metadata in the AWS Cloud, but it does not provide an interactive development environment or real-time data processing capabilities.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>AWS Glue Crawlers&nbsp;</strong></span>is incorrect because while AWS Glue Crawlers can automatically discover new data and extract metadata, they do not provide an interactive development environment or real-time data processing capabilities.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/notebook-getting-started.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/notebook-getting-started.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/interactively-develop-your-aws-glue-streaming-etl-jobs-using-aws-glue-studio-notebooks/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/interactively-develop-your-aws-glue-streaming-etl-jobs-using-aws-glue-studio-notebooks/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-data-integration-jobs-with-ai-companion-on-aws-glue-studio-notebook-powered-by-amazon-codewhisperer/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-data-integration-jobs-with-ai-companion-on-aws-glue-studio-notebook-powered-by-amazon-codewhisperer/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":9,"sourceNumber":18,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A data engineer is working on a project that involves processing large datasets using Python. The project requires a service that can handle distributed processing over multiple nodes with the least amount of operational overhead. The service should also be capable of handling both batch and real-time data processing tasks.</p>\n<p>Which of the following AWS services would be the most suitable for this task?</p>","answers":["AWS Glue for Ray","AWS Lambda","Amazon EMR (Elastic MapReduce)","AWS Glue DataBrew"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p><strong>AWS Glue for Ray</strong> is a job type in AWS Glue, a serverless data integration service. It’s designed to scale AI and Python workloads. AWS Glue for Ray allows you to scale your Python workloads in a distributed cluster in a multi-node environment. This is particularly useful when you have large datasets that can’t fit on a single instance.</p>\n<p>Ray is an open-source unified compute framework that originated as a project at RISELab in UC Berkeley. It’s designed to scale AI and Python workloads. If your application is written in Python, you can scale it with Ray in a distributed cluster. Ray is Python native and can be combined with the AWS SDK for pandas to prepare, integrate, and transform your data for running your data analytics and ML workloads.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/23962111c258c93490315c443d1bae8880b40a86d4f8687654883f329c07ac85.jpg\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>You can use AWS Glue for Ray with Glue Studio Notebooks, SageMaker Studio Notebook, or a local notebook or IDE of your choice. This makes it a flexible and powerful tool for scaling Python workloads.</p>\n<p><strong>AWS Glue for Ray consists of two major components:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Ray Core – The distributed computing framework.</li>\n<li>Ray Dataset – The distributed data framework based on Apache Arrow.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>When running a Ray job, AWS Glue provisions the Ray cluster for you and runs these distributed Python jobs on a serverless auto-scaling infrastructure. This means you don’t have to worry about managing the underlying infrastructure, and you can focus on writing and running your Python workloads.</p>\n<p>Developers like Python for its ease of use and rich collection of built-in data processing libraries. They want to use familiar Python primitive type for processing large datasets. AWS Glue for Ray helps data engineers process large datasets using Python and popular Python libraries. AWS Glue for Ray uses&nbsp;Ray.io, an open-source unified compute framework that helps scale Python workloads from a single node to hundreds of nodes. AWS Glue for Ray is serverless, so there is no infrastructure to manage.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is&nbsp;<span><strong>AWS Glue for Ray.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span><strong>AWS Lambda</strong></span> is incorrect. It<strong>&nbsp;</strong>is a serverless computing service that lets you run your code without provisioning or managing servers. While it’s great for running small, independent tasks in response to cloud events (like changes to data in an S3 bucket or updates to a DynamoDB table), it may not be the best choice for processing large datasets using Python. AWS Lambda has a maximum execution time of 15 minutes per request, which might not be sufficient for processing large datasets.</p>\n<p><strong><span>Amazon EMR (Elastic MapReduce)</span></strong>&nbsp;is incorrect. EMR is a cloud-based big data platform for processing large datasets using popular frameworks such as Apache Spark and Hadoop. While it’s powerful and flexible, it requires a fair amount of setup and management, which might not be ideal for a Python-based data processing task.</p>\n<p><strong><span>AWS Glue DataBrew</span></strong>&nbsp;is incorrect because AWS Glue DataBrew is a visual data preparation tool that makes it easy to clean and normalize data for analytics and machine learning. It’s a great tool for data cleaning and exploration, but it’s not specifically designed for processing large datasets using Python.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-for-ray-scaling-your-data-integration-workloads-using-python/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-for-ray-scaling-your-data-integration-workloads-using-python/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/glue/features/\">https://aws.amazon.com/glue/features/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":10,"sourceNumber":21,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p><span>A finance company stores transaction records in <span><span><span>CSV</span></span></span> format within an Amazon S3 bucket, protected using dual-layer server-side encryption with AWS <span><span><span>KMS</span></span></span> keys (DSSE-<span>KMS</span>). The data analytics team rarely queries an entire record, typically requiring only 1-3 columns for analysis. The company wants to minimize the costs related to its query operations.</span></p>\n<p><span>Which solution would meet the requirement?</span></p>","answers":["Implement an AWS Glue PySpark job to process and transform data from CSV to JSON format.","<span>Utilize the <code>NEST_TO_MAP</code> transform in AWS Glue DataBrew to convert CSV files to JSON format before storing them in the data lake.</span>","Configure an AWS Glue PySpark job to process the source data and ingest it into the S3 data lake in Apache Avro format.","Launch an AWS Glue extract, transform, and load (ETL) job to transform the CSV data into Apache Parquet format."],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p><strong>AWS Glue</strong>&nbsp;is a powerful ETL service that easily moves data between different data stores. By using AWS Glue, you can easily create and manage ETL jobs to transfer data from various sources, such as Amazon S3, Amazon RDS, and Amazon Redshift. Additionally, AWS Glue enables you to transform your data as needed to fit your specific needs. One of the key advantages of using AWS Glue is that data engineers can automate the time-consuming steps of data preparation for analytics and data storage, such as loading data from various sources, cleaning, normalizing data, and combining it into a data lake or a relational database for analysis.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/eea57a805f71d4d36a080098419482dd7db72940773575d04a5cbba405c3025d.png\" alt=\"AWS Glue\"></p>\n<p>When working with big data processing, it is often necessary to transform data from one format to another to optimize processing efficiency. Apache Parquet is a columnar storage format designed to provide higher efficiency and performance for big data processing. By storing and processing large amounts of data with high compression rates and faster query times, Parquet can offer significant benefits to the company. Fortunately, Apache Parquet is compatible with many data processing frameworks such as Spark, Hive, and Hadoop, making it a versatile format for big data processing. Moreover, by using AWS Glue and other AWS services, companies can easily convert their .csv files to the more efficient Apache Parquet format and store the output files in an S3 bucket, making it easy to access and process large amounts of data. This efficiency reduces storage costs and improves performance when querying the data, making it an ideal choice for analytical queries that do not need to scan the entire dataset, such as those typically performed with Amazon Athena.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Launch an AWS Glue extract, transform, and load (ETL) job to transform the CSV data into Apache Parquet format.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says:<span> <strong>Implement an AWS Glue PySpark job to process and transform data from CSV to JSON format</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. Transforming data from CSV to JSON would not meet the requirement of minimizing query costs. JSON is a row-oriented format, which means querying a few columns out of many would still require processing the entire dataset.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Utilize the <code>NEST_TO_MAP</code> transform in AWS Glue DataBrew to convert CSV files to JSON format before storing them in the data lake</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. Similar to the other incorrect option, JSON, being row-oriented, is not suitable for scenarios where only a small subset of columns is queried frequently.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <strong><span>Configure an AWS Glue PySpark job to process the source data and ingest it into the S3 data lake in Apache Avro format</span></strong>&nbsp;is incorrect.&nbsp;Apache Avro is a file format primarily designed to store data in rows and support schema evolution. This row-based nature of Apache Avro means that more data needs to be scanned during a query compared to columnar formats. This feature makes it an excellent choice for data likely to undergo schema changes over time. However, when it comes to running analytical queries that focus on a small set of columns from a large dataset, Apache Avro may not be as efficient as columnar formats like Apache Parquet.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-format-parquet-home.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-format-parquet-home.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/glue-athena.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/glue-athena.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/three-aws-glue-etl-job-types-for-converting-data-to-apache-parquet.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/three-aws-glue-etl-job-types-for-converting-data-to-apache-parquet.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":11,"sourceNumber":24,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A company is developing a new data analysis application. The application needs to retrieve and process data from an Amazon S3 bucket. The data access patterns are complex and vary based on the analysis being performed. The company wants to use a service that allows them to use their own code to process and transform the data as it is being retrieved from Amazon S3.</p>\n<p>Which AWS service should the company use to meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["AWS Glue","AWS Lambda","Amazon S3 Storage Lens","Amazon S3 Object Lambda"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p><strong>Amazon S3 Object Lambda</strong> allows you to add your own code to Amazon S3 GET, LIST, and HEAD requests to process and modify data as it is being returned to an application. This enables the use of custom code to transform the data returned by these requests in various ways, such as filtering rows, dynamically resizing and watermarking images, or redacting confidential data. This service is powered by AWS Lambda functions, which means your code runs on fully managed AWS infrastructure. This eliminates the need to create and store derivative copies of your data or to run proxies, with no changes required to your applications.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/ce313ec97c10343e28dfc645c0f5750e08f67bb9f0855980949df734d620f748.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>S3 Object Lambda operates by using AWS Lambda functions to automatically process the output of standard S3 <code>GET</code>, <code>LIST</code>, or <code>HEAD</code> requests. You can author and run your own custom Lambda functions, allowing you to tailor the data transformation to your specific use cases. Once you configure a Lambda function, you attach it to an S3 Object Lambda service endpoint, known as an Object Lambda Access Point. When a request is sent to your Object Lambda Access Point, Amazon S3 automatically calls your Lambda function. Any data retrieved using an S3 <code>GET</code>, <code>LIST</code>, or <code>HEAD</code> request through the Object Lambda Access Point is then returned as a transformed result back to the application.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Amazon S3 Object Lambda.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>AWS Glue</strong></span> is incorrect. While AWS Glue does provide capabilities for data transformation, it does not allow for processing data as it is being retrieved from S3. It is more focused on preparing and combining data for analytics.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <strong><span>AWS Lambda</span> </strong>is incorrect. A Lambda function alone does not provide the functionality to process and transform data during its retrieval from S3 without integrating with other services like S3 Object Lambda.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Amazon S3 Storage Lens</strong></span> is incorrect because S3 Storage Lens is just a cloud-storage analytics feature that you can use to gain organization-wide visibility into object-storage usage and activity. It does not allow for processing data as it is being retrieved from S3. It’s more focused on providing insights about storage usage and helping you optimize costs.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/features/object-lambda/\">https://aws.amazon.com/s3/features/object-lambda/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/transforming-objects.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/transforming-objects.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/introducing-amazon-s3-object-lambda-use-your-code-to-process-data-as-it-is-being-retrieved-from-s3/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/introducing-amazon-s3-object-lambda-use-your-code-to-process-data-as-it-is-being-retrieved-from-s3/</a></p>\n<p><strong>Check out these Amazon S3 and AWS Lambda Cheat Sheets:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":12,"sourceNumber":25,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>An e-commerce company has a vast amount of data stored in Amazon S3 buckets. The data is in various formats and is updated frequently. The company needs to orchestrate an ETL pipeline to transform and load this data into Amazon Redshift for further analysis. The solution should be able to handle changes in the data schema and should be easily manageable.</p>\n<p>Which AWS service is best suited for these requirements?</p>","answers":["AWS Step Functions","Amazon Data Firehose","AWS Glue Workflows","Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA)"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Extract, transform, and load (ETL) orchestration is a common mechanism for building big data pipelines. Orchestration for parallel ETL processing requires the use of multiple tools to perform a variety of operations. To simplify the orchestration, you can use&nbsp;AWS Glue&nbsp;workflows.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/83e328a7398015d305e09a9016fce5824abfad33624b45e80d39147bfdbb3bff.png\">AWS Glue workflows&nbsp;provide a visual and programmatic tool to author data pipelines by combining AWS Glue crawlers for schema discovery and AWS Glue Spark and Python shell jobs to transform the data. A workflow consists of one or more task nodes arranged as a graph. Relationships can be defined, and parameters passed between task nodes to enable you to build pipelines of varying complexity. You can trigger workflows on a schedule or on-demand. You can track the progress of each node independently or the entire workflow, making it easier to troubleshoot your pipelines.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>AWS Glue Workflows.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>AWS Step Functions</strong></span> is incorrect.&nbsp;Step Functions is a viable option as it can use the AWS SDK to call Glue APIs. However, compared to a Glue Workflow, which is specifically designed for orchestrating Glue Crawlers and Glue Jobs, Step Functions is less straightforward. For example, when you run a crawler in Step Functions, you're responsible for monitoring its status and implementing logic to poll whether the Crawler has completed before proceeding to the next step. In contrast, Glue Workflows handle monitoring of Jobs and Crawlers automatically.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Amazon Data Firehose</strong></span> is incorrect because it is only designed for streaming data into destinations such as Amazon S3, Amazon Redshift, and Amazon OpenSearch Service. It is not an ETL tool and does not handle data transformations or schema changes efficiently.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA)</strong></span> is incorrect because, while it can be used to manage complex ETL workflows, it primarily serves as a platform to programmatically author, schedule, and monitor workflows. It does not have the same level of integration with AWS Glue or the ability to handle changes in the data schema as effectively as AWS Glue workflows. It is more suited for custom workflows that require a high level of customization and flexibility.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/orchestrate-an-etl-pipeline-using-aws-glue-workflows-triggers-and-crawlers-with-custom-classifiers/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/orchestrate-an-etl-pipeline-using-aws-glue-workflows-triggers-and-crawlers-with-custom-classifiers/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":13,"sourceNumber":26,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A data engineering team is working on a new project that involves processing and analyzing a large amount of data. The data is collected from various sources and is expected to grow exponentially.</p>\n<p>The team needs a solution that can handle this scale of data and provide efficient querying capabilities. They also need to ensure centralized data governance and access control.</p>\n<p>Which combination of AWS services would best meet the team’s requirements? (Select TWO.)</p>","answers":["Utilize Amazon DynamoDB to store data and Amazon QuickSight to create visualizations and perform ad-hoc analysis of this data.","Utilize Amazon S3 to store data and Amazon Athena for running analysis.","Utilize Amazon Redshift to store and analyze a large amount of data and AWS Glue to prepare the data (ETL operations) for analytics.","Utilize AWS Glue Data Catalog to centralize data access and permissions.","Utilize Lake Formation to centralize data access and permissions."],"correct":[1,4],"explanation":"<p>Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) is storage for the internet. You can use Amazon S3 to store and retrieve any amount of data at any time, from anywhere on the web.</p>\n<p>Amazon Athena is a serverless, interactive analytics service built on open-source frameworks that support open-table and file formats. Athena provides a simplified, flexible way to analyze petabytes of data where it lives. Analyze data or build applications from an Amazon Simple Storage Service (S3) data lake and 30 data sources, including on-premises data sources or other cloud systems using SQL or Python. Athena is built on open-source Trino and Presto engines and Apache Spark frameworks, with no provisioning or configuration effort required.</p>\n<p>AWS Lake Formation is a managed service that makes it easy to set up, secure, and manage your data lakes. Lake Formation helps you discover your data sources and then catalog, cleanse, and transform the data. You can use Lake Formation to secure the data and ingest it into an Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) data lake.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/0ede9de7a9e531200693688d368d4d377b4abd2baa2fd73bb32c98532d8e71a5.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>Amazon S3 is a scalable object storage service that can handle large amounts of unstructured data, which is ideal for the team’s requirement of handling an exponentially growing amount of data.</p>\n<p>Amazon Athena is a serverless interactive query service that makes it easy to analyze data in Amazon S3 using standard SQL. It can handle large-scale datasets, which aligns with the team’s need for efficient querying capabilities.</p>\n<p>AWS Lake Formation is a service that makes it easy to set up, secure, and manage data lakes. It provides centralized data governance and access control, which is one of the team’s requirements.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answers are:</p>\n<p><span><strong>- Utilize Amazon S3 to store data and Amazon Athena for running analysis.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span><strong>- Utilize Lake Formation to centralize data access and permissions. </strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Utilize Amazon DynamoDB to store data and Amazon QuickSight to create visualizations and perform ad-hoc analysis of this data</strong></span>&nbsp;is&nbsp;incorrect. While DynamoDB is excellent for handling large amounts of data, it’s primarily a NoSQL database service used for key-value and document data structures. It might not be the best choice for complex analytical queries.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Utilize Amazon Redshift to store and analyze a large amount of data and AWS Glue to prepare the data (ETL operations) for analytics</strong></span>&nbsp;is&nbsp;incorrect&nbsp;because while Amazon Redshift is indeed a powerful data warehousing solution, it’s primarily designed for structured data and might not be the best fit for handling unstructured or semi-structured data that could come from various sources in this scenario. AWS Glue, while great for ETL operations, doesn’t provide the storage or querying capabilities needed for this scenario. It’s more about data preparation rather than data storage or querying.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Utilize AWS Glue Data Catalog to centralize data access and permissions. </strong></span>&nbsp;is&nbsp;incorrect. Glue Data Catalog just serves as a centrailize&nbsp;metadata store. By itself, it's not capable of&nbsp;managing data access permissions or governance.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/s3/\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/s3/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/athena/\">https://aws.amazon.com/athena/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out these Amazon S3, Amazon Athena, and AWS Lake Formation Cheat Sheets:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":14,"sourceNumber":28,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A team of data engineers is developing an ETL (Extract, Transform, Load) pipeline that will use an Amazon S3-based data lake as its source. Sensitive information has been discovered during data exploration.</p>\n<p>To ensure data privacy and protection, the data engineering team must implement a mechanism that can identify and redact the Personally Identifiable Information (PII) before the data is ingested.</p>\n<p>Which combination of steps should be implemented to meet these requirements with the LEAST amount of development effort? (Select TWO.)</p>","answers":["Implement an Amazon Data Firehose stream with AWS Lambda for PII redaction.","Create a Glue job in AWS Glue Studio and use the Detect PII transform to identify and redact columns containing PII.","Create a rule in AWS Glue Data Quality to determine and redact the PII.","Use AWS Macie for PII identification and data redaction.","Leverage AWS Step Functions to orchestrate the ingestion and processing workflow of the dataset."],"correct":[1,4],"explanation":"<p><strong>AWS Glue Studio</strong> offers a no-code visual interface for data integration tasks, including the detection and redaction of PII from datasets before they are ingested into a data lake. This approach minimizes the operational effort as it leverages AWS's managed services to handle both the detection of sensitive information and the orchestration of data workflows. AWS Glue's PII transform feature automatically identifies and masks or redacts sensitive data, ensuring that any PII present in the source data is appropriately handled before it reaches the S3 data lake.</p>\n<p><strong>AWS Step Functions</strong> can then be used to orchestrate the workflow, controlling the flow of data from its source through the AWS Glue Studio for PII redaction and finally into the Amazon S3 data lake. This service provides a robust mechanism for managing complex data-processing workflows with minimal manual intervention, reducing operational burden.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/0b0874b7bf4f6bb2bffe6c2fefef51fa43ff113174c9fb4c94d9ae881ab60003.png\"></p>\n<p>In this scenario, the company can use AWS Glue Studio to visually create a Glue job to identify and redact PII from datasets. AWS Glue Studio includes a built-in Detect PII transform that the company can use right away. Glue Studio will automatically generate the necessary code for the job. Once done, you can include the Glue job as part of the pipeline orchestrated by an AWS Step Functions workflow.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answers are:</p>\n<p><span><strong>- Create a Glue job in AWS Glue Studio and use the Detect PII transform to identify and redact columns containing PII.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span><strong>- Leverage AWS Step Functions to orchestrate the ingestion and processing workflow of the dataset.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Implement an Amazon Data Firehose stream with AWS Lambda for PII redaction</strong></span> is incorrect. While feasible, this approach requires custom development and management of a Lambda function for PII detection and redaction, potentially increasing operational effort and complexity.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Create a rule in AWS Glue Data Quality to determine and redact the PII</strong></span> is incorrect because AWS Glue Data Quality primarily focuses on monitoring data quality over time rather than performing data transformation tasks like PII redaction.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Use AWS Macie for PII identification and data redaction</strong></span> is incorrect because AWS Macie is primarily a data security and data privacy service focused on identifying and protecting sensitive data in AWS. While it can identify PII, it doesn't directly support redaction or data transformation as part of an ETL process like AWS Glue Studio.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/detect-PII.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/detect-PII.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-data-pipeline-to-automatically-discover-and-mask-pii-data-with-aws-glue-databrew/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-data-pipeline-to-automatically-discover-and-mask-pii-data-with-aws-glue-databrew/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:</strong><br>\n<a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":15,"sourceNumber":30,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p><span>A data engineering team must perform data transformation of CSV files uploaded to an Amazon S3 bucket. An AWS Lambda function is ready to process these files. The team wants to invoke the Lambda function automatically whenever a new CSV file is uploaded to the S3 bucket.</span></p>\n<p><span>Which action would meet the requirements in the LEAST amount of development effort? (Select TWO.)</span></p>","answers":["<span>Use S3 Event Notifications to trigger the Lambda function when the <code>s3:ObjectTagging:*</code> event type occurs. Specify the Lambda function as the destination for publishing the event.</span>","Configure S3 Event Notifications to send a message to an Amazon SQS queue when a new CSV file is uploaded. Then, configure the Lambda function to be directly triggered by this SQS queue.","<span>Use Amazon EventBridge to trigger the Lambda function when the <code>s3:ObjectCreated:*</code> event type occurs. Implement a code in the function that filters out files not ending with <code>.csv</code></span>","<span>Use S3 Event Notifications to trigger the Lambda function when the <code>s3:ObjectCreated:*</code> event type occurs. Specify the Lambda function as the destination for publishing the event.</span>","<span>Create an S3 Event Notifications with a filter rule to send notifications when the uploaded file ends with <code>.csv</code></span>"],"correct":[3,4],"explanation":"<p>The Amazon S3 Event Notifications functionality allows you to be alerted when specific occurrences take place in your S3 bucket. By setting up a notification configuration, you can specify which events should trigger Amazon S3 to send out notifications.&nbsp;Additionally, you need to designate where these notifications should be delivered.&nbsp;This configuration is stored in the&nbsp;<em>notification</em>&nbsp;subresource that's associated with a bucket.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/2cf83ab5bc2b2c2482bdae7173cda36507af22e0d11146f6c066c54c209e5452.png\" alt=\"Amazon S3 Event notification\"></p>\n<p>The&nbsp;<code>s3:ObjectCreated:*</code>&nbsp;event type includes all object creation events, such as uploading a new CSV file. When this event occurs, the S3 Event Notification feature triggers the specified AWS Lambda function. The Lambda function can then process the CSV file as needed.</p>\n<p>Notifications can be configured to trigger based on the prefix and suffix of an object's key name. For instance, you can set up a rule to receive notifications only when image files with a \".jpg\" extension are added to a bucket. Alternatively, you can configure notifications to be sent to an Amazon SNS topic when objects with the prefix \"images/\" are added, while directing notifications for objects prefixed with \"logs/\" to an AWS Lambda function within the same bucket.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answers are:</p>\n<p><span><strong>-</strong> <strong>Use S3 Event Notifications to trigger the Lambda function when the&nbsp;<code>s3:ObjectCreated:*</code>&nbsp;event type occurs. Specify the Lambda function as the destination for publishing the event.&nbsp;</strong></span>This is the most straightforward solution and requires minimal development effort. Configuring Amazon S3 Event Notifications with the <code>s3:ObjectCreated:*</code> event type directly triggers the Lambda function whenever an object is uploaded to the S3 bucket. There’s no need to build complex filtering logic, as the event will be published directly to Lambda, eliminating any additional overhead.</p>\n<p><span><strong>- Create an S3 Event Notifications with a filter rule to send notifications when the uploaded file ends with <code>.csv.&nbsp;</code></strong></span>This option adds a filter to ensure that only CSV files trigger the notification. This solution directly meets the requirement of triggering the Lambda function for CSV files, without the need for additional filtering logic in the Lambda function.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <strong><span> Use S3 Event Notifications to trigger the Lambda function when the <code>s3:ObjectTagging:*</code> event type occurs. Specify the Lambda function as the destination for publishing the event</span></strong>&nbsp;is&nbsp;incorrect&nbsp;because the&nbsp;<code>s3:ObjectTagging:*</code>&nbsp;event type is primarily triggered when an object’s tags are created, deleted, or updated, not when a new object is created.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Configure S3 Event Notifications to send a message to an Amazon SQS queue when a new CSV file is uploaded. Then, configure the Lambda function to be directly triggered by this SQS queue</strong></span>&nbsp;is&nbsp;incorrect&nbsp;because while this approach would work, it typically involves more development effort than necessary because it introduces an additional component (SQS) into the architecture.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use Amazon EventBridge to trigger the Lambda function when the <code>s3:ObjectCreated:*</code> event type occurs. Implement a code in the function that filters out files not ending with <code>.csv</code>&nbsp;</strong></span>is&nbsp;incorrect.&nbsp;This adds development effort because you need to manually code and maintain the filtering logic for <code>.csv</code> files, whereas S3 Event Notifications can be configured to filter events without code.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-s3-example.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-s3-example.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/EventNotifications.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/EventNotifications.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/notification-how-to-filtering.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/notification-how-to-filtering.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon S3 Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":16,"sourceNumber":31,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A company operates two Amazon Redshift clusters. The company’s Analytics Team uses the first cluster to derive critical business insights, while the Reporting Team uses the second cluster for report generation.</p>\n<p>The Reporting team is tasked with publishing near real-time reports on an Amazon QuickSight dashboard. To achieve this, they require joining data from their cluster with specific datasets in the Analytics Team’s cluster.</p>\n<p>Which solution will satisfy these criteria with the LEAST impact on the Analytic’s team cluster?</p>","answers":["Configure an AWS Database Migration Service replication with the Analytics team's cluster as the source and the Reporting team's as the target.","Configure Amazon Redshift Data Sharing with the Reporting team's cluster as the consumer.","In the Analytics team's cluster, create a materialized view of the required tables and give the Reporting team access.","In the Reporting team’s cluster, create an Amazon Redshift Spectrum table using the unloaded copy of data from the Analytics team's cluster to an Amazon S3 bucket."],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Amazon Redshift is a fully managed, petabyte-scale data warehouse service in the cloud. The Amazon Redshift service manages all of the work of setting up, operating, and scaling a data warehouse. These tasks include provisioning capacity, monitoring and backing up the cluster and applying patches and upgrades to the Amazon Redshift engine.</p>\n<p>Amazon Redshift can also securely share read access to live data across Amazon Redshift clusters, workgroups, AWS accounts, and AWS Regions without manually moving or copying the data with <strong>Amazon Redshift data sharing</strong>. Since the data is live, all users can see the most up-to-date and consistent information in Amazon Redshift as soon as it’s updated.</p>\n<p><strong>Amazon Redshift data sharing Use cases:</strong></p>\n<p>- Supporting different kinds of business-critical workloads</p>\n<p>- Enable seamless collaboration across teams and business groups for broader analytics, data science, and cross-product impact analysis.</p>\n<p>- Delivering data as a service</p>\n<p>- Sharing data between environments</p>\n<p>- Licensing access to data in Amazon Redshift</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/137b817b78e2fd7ce40087a7cfa2c339c76fd0124ca1d2b21fce261b97867f61.png\" alt=\"https://media.tutorialsdojo.com/public/Redshuft Data Sharing .png\"></p>\n<p>Amazon Redshift data sharing allows you to securely and easily share data for read purposes across different Amazon Redshift clusters without the complexity and delays associated with data copies and data movement. Data can be shared at many levels, including schemas, tables, views, and user-defined functions, providing fine-grained access controls that can be tailored for different users and businesses that all need access to the data.</p>\n<p>With this feature, you can seamlessly share live data between clusters. This option suits the requirement in the scenario, as the shared data will be made available to the consumer as quickly as possible. It will ensure transactional consistency and provide up-to-date and consistent data views to the Reporting team.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Configure Amazon Redshift Data Sharing with the Reporting team's cluster as the consumer.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Configure an AWS Database Migration Service replication with the Analytics team's cluster as the source and the Reporting team's as the target</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. While this method can replicate data from one Redshift cluster to another, this approach involves moving data continuously, which can add unnecessary load and complexity to the Analytics team's cluster. This could potentially impact the performance of the analytics operations, especially if the data volumes are large.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>In the Analytics team's cluster, create a materialized view of the required tables and give the Reporting team access&nbsp;</strong></span>is incorrect. Creating a materialized view in the Analytics team's cluster could potentially impact the cluster's performance since it requires additional storage and computational resources to maintain the view, especially if the data changes frequently.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong> In the Reporting team’s cluster, create an Amazon Redshift Spectrum table using the unloaded copy of data from the Analytics team's cluster to an Amazon S3 bucket</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. Even though this will allow the Reporting team to query the same data from the Analytics cluster, it still will not satisfy the requirement, as unloading data to an S3 bucket could lead to delays in data availability for real-time reporting. Moreover, the unload operation imposes an additional workload on the Analytics team's cluster.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/announcing-amazon-redshift-data-sharing-preview/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/announcing-amazon-redshift-data-sharing-preview/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/data_sharing_intro.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/data_sharing_intro.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/datashare-overview.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/datashare-overview.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":17,"sourceNumber":32,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A company uses an Amazon Redshift cluster to store and analyze weekly sales data. To enhance its analytics, the company plans to perform SQL joins between streaming transaction data and the data within the Redshift cluster.</p>\n<p>This transaction data is ingested into an Amazon Kinesis Data Stream at a rate of several megabytes per second. The company aims to visualize these joined datasets in Amazon QuickSight to derive insights in near real-time.</p>\n<p>Which set of steps can satisfy the requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?</p>","answers":["<span>Configure Kinesis Data Streams as the source and Amazon S3 as the destination. Load the data from Amazon S3 to Amazon Redshift by running the <code>COPY</code> command.</span>","<span>Configure the Kinesis Data Streams as the source and the Kinesis Firehose as the destination. Deliver data with Kinesis Firehose to an Amazon S3 bucket and issue an Amazon Redshift <code>COPY</code> command.</span>","Configure streaming ingestion by creating an external schema mapping the data stream. Then, create a materialized view referencing the external schema with auto refresh.","<span>Configure Kinesis Data Streams to load data using SQL queries. Create materialized views with auto refresh set to <code>YES</code> using stream data.</span>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>You can use <strong>Amazon Kinesis Data Streams</strong> to collect and process large streams of data records in real-time. You can send the processed records to dashboards, use them to generate alerts, dynamically change pricing and advertising strategies, or send data to a variety of other AWS services.</p>\n<p><strong>Amazon Redshift</strong> is a fully managed, petabyte-scale data warehouse service in the cloud. You can load data and start querying right away in the Amazon Redshift query editor v2 or in your favorite business intelligence (BI) tool. Enjoy the best price performance and familiar SQL features in an easy-to-use, zero administration environment.</p>\n<p>Amazon Redshift supports streaming ingestion from Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. Streaming ingestion provides low-latency, high-speed ingestion of stream data from Amazon Kinesis Data Streams into an Amazon Redshift provisioned or Amazon Redshift Serverless materialized view. It lowers the time it takes to access data, and it reduces storage cost.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/0b758707e518813fa73165234627c86f8976a003ad4e929cd585aae0c1859108.png\" alt=\"\"><em>Sample Architecture of Streaming Ingestion with Amazon Kinesis Data Stream and Amazon Redshift. The source for this architecture is coming from DynamoDB with data streaming turned on.</em></p>\n<p>These are the steps in configuring a streaming ingestion, assuming you already have an existing Kinesis Data Stream:</p>\n<p>1. In Amazon Redshift, create an external schema to map the data from Kinesis to a schema.</p>\n<pre><code>CREATE EXTERNAL SCHEMA kds\nFROM KINESIS\nREGION 'us-west-2'\nIAM_ROLE { default | 'iam-role-arn' };</code></pre>\n<p>2. Create a materialized view to consume the stream data. The following example defines a materialized view with JSON source data. <strong>Note:&nbsp;</strong>To turn on auto-refresh, use&nbsp;<code>AUTO REFRESH YES</code>. The default behavior is manual refresh.</p>\n<pre><code>CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW my_view AUTO REFRESH YES AS\nSELECT approximate_arrival_timestamp,\nJSON_PARSE(kinesis_data) as Data\nFROM kds.my_stream_name\nWHERE CAN_JSON_PARSE(kinesis_data);</code></pre>\n<p>3. Refresh the view, which invokes Redshift to read from the stream and load data into the materialized view.</p>\n<pre><code>REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW my_view;</code></pre>\n<p>4. Query data in the materialized view.</p>\n<p>By following the steps above, you can directly ingest the data from Kinesis Data Stream to Amazon Redshift without the need to stage data. This will satisfy the requirements for adding real-time capabilities to the company's setup.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Configure streaming ingestion by creating an external schema mapping the data stream. Then, create a materialized view referencing the external schema with auto refresh.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Configure Kinesis Data Streams as the source and Amazon S3 as the destination. Load the data from Amazon S3 to Amazon Redshift by running the <code>COPY</code> command</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. The Kinesis Data Stream does not have built-in support for directly loading data into Amazon S3. This approach is only technically possible using other AWS services with Kinesis Data Stream, such as AWS Lambda or Kinesis Data Firehose. While loading data using the COPY command can deliver data to Amazon Redshift, it is primarily used for bulk data loading, not for real-time applications. Hence, this solution cannot satisfy the requirements.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Configure the Kinesis Data Streams as the source and the Kinesis Firehose as the destination. Deliver data with Kinesis Firehose to an Amazon S3 bucket and issue an Amazon Redshift <code>COPY</code> command</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. While it is a viable approach, this method introduces delays since the data is first staged in Amazon S3 and then loaded into the Amazon Redshift cluster. This additional step largely impacts the goal of achieving near real-time analytics due to the time required to move data through these stages.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Configure Kinesis Data Streams to load data using SQL queries. Create materialized views with auto refresh set to <code>YES</code> using stream data&nbsp;</strong></span>is incorrect because running SQL queries directly in the Kinesis Data Streams is impossible. Kinesis Data Streams is a managed service provided by AWS that allows you to build custom applications that process or analyze streaming data in real time. It does not support running SQL queries directly.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/near-real-time-analytics-using-amazon-redshift-streaming-ingestion-with-amazon-kinesis-data-streams-and-amazon-dynamodb/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/near-real-time-analytics-using-amazon-redshift-streaming-ingestion-with-amazon-kinesis-data-streams-and-amazon-dynamodb/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion-getting-started.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion-getting-started.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":18,"sourceNumber":33,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A company wants to conduct real-time analysis of streaming data from various sources. The system must be fault-tolerant to ensure reliability and continuous operation. The company intends to continuously aggregate data from the most recent 5-minute period to gain actionable insights.</p>\n<p>Which of the following options would meet the requirements with the LEAST development effort?</p>","answers":["Launch an Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink to analyze streaming data. Use a sliding window query for time-based aggregations to continuously aggregate data.","Configure an Apache Kafka with custom Java applications for real-time data processing.","Enable the Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink Studio for SQL-based real-time analytics. Aggregate data using a tumbling window query for time-based aggregations.","Utilize Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with AWS Lambda for real-time data processing over large, distributed data streams."],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>With Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink, you can use Java, Scala, Python, or SQL to process and analyze streaming data. The service enables you to author and run code against streaming sources and static sources to perform time-series analytics and feed real-time dashboards and metrics.</p>\n<p>You can build applications in Managed Service for Apache Flink with the language of your choice using open-source libraries based on&nbsp;Apache Flink. Apache Flink is a popular framework and engine for processing data streams.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/6f0f7247f234bcff91d1525b597c5850cda33bdb16a22321859cda8708b88828.png\" alt=\"Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink\"></p>\n<p>Managed Service for Apache Flink provides the underlying infrastructure for your Apache Flink applications. It handles core capabilities like provisioning compute resources, AZ failover resilience, parallel computation, automatic scaling, and application backups (implemented as checkpoints and snapshots). You can use the high-level Flink programming features (such as operators, functions, sources, and sinks) in the same way that you use them when hosting the Flink infrastructure yourself.</p>\n<p>For handling large volumes of streaming data with requirements for real-time analysis, fault tolerance, and the capability to perform diverse aggregation tasks over time windows, Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink is a robust solution. This service is specifically designed for processing and analyzing streams of data in real time, offering the scalability, flexibility, and fault tolerance needed for such tasks. Apache Flink's support for different types of windowing queries, including sliding windows, and tumbling windows. Sliding window aggregations allow for analyzing data across overlapping time windows, which is ideal for continuous monitoring and analysis no longer than one hour. This window query is suitable for continuous, real-time analysis over the most recent period. Moreover, the tumbling windows&nbsp;are fixed-sized, non-overlapping intervals of time. Each event in a data stream belongs to exactly one window. This window is ideal for scenarios where you need to perform an operation over a chunk of data collected within a regular interval, and each interval is considered independent. For example, counting the number of events every minute.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Launch an Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink to analyze streaming data. Use a sliding window query for time-based aggregations to continuously aggregate data.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Configure an Apache Kafka with custom Java applications for real-time data processing</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because managing a Kafka cluster and developing custom applications to process the data is more complex and time-consuming than using a managed service.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Enable the Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink Studio for SQL-based real-time analytics. Aggregate data using a tumbling window query for time-based aggregations</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because the tumbling window primarily aggregates data over non-overlapping, fixed-sized time intervals. This does not fulfill the requirement to aggregate over the most recent 5-minute periods continuously.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Utilize Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with AWS Lambda for real-time data processing over large, distributed data streams</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. Although AWS Lambda can typically process data from Kinesis Data Streams, this setup is not as straightforward and complicated to implement compared to a dedicated streaming service like Flink. Moreover, Lambda functions have an execution duration limit per request that is not suitable for continuous long-term processing of streaming data.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/managed-flink/latest/java/what-is.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/managed-flink/latest/java/what-is.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kinesisanalytics/latest/dev/sliding-window-concepts.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kinesisanalytics/latest/dev/sliding-window-concepts.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/managed-flink/latest/java/how-fault.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/managed-flink/latest/java/how-fault.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out these Comparison of AWS Services Cheat Sheets:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/comparison-of-aws-services/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/comparison-of-aws-services/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":19,"sourceNumber":36,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A data engineering team at a media company is developing a new feature for their application that allows users to upload videos. Once a video is uploaded to their Amazon S3 bucket, a process must be triggered immediately to transcode it for different devices. The team has decided to use the <code>s3:ObjectCreated</code> action as the event trigger.</p>\n<p>Which of the following options is the most efficient way to set up this architecture?</p>","answers":["Configure Amazon S3 to publish an event with the video's S3 path to an Amazon SQS queue when a new file is uploaded. Then, have the transcoding service poll the queue.","Set up S3 Event Notifications to send a message to an AWS Lambda function whenever a new object is created in the bucket, and then have the Lambda function trigger the transcoding service.","Use AWS Lambda to continuously poll the S3 bucket for new files and trigger the transcoding service when a new file is found.","Use Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to stream the data from the S3 bucket to AWS Lambda, and then have Lambda trigger the transcoding service when a new file is detected."],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Amazon S3 provides a useful feature called 'Event Notifications', which enables you to receive notifications when specific events occur in your S3 bucket. To use this feature, you need to create a notification configuration that specifies the events you want to track and the destinations where you want to receive the notifications.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/73fe4ef0a3a70ad8150db51eb850ba66439982075f5608e75b55e43dae1c1334.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>AWS Lambda can be used to process event notifications from Amazon Simple Storage Service. By configuring notification settings on a bucket, Amazon S3 can send an event to a Lambda function when an object is created or deleted. To enable this functionality, you must grant Amazon S3 permission to invoke a function using the function's resource-based permissions policy.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/93df42455c606dd452507951b6cf80345e2cf83b43b2e1dee93a38aa8cc13dd4.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>Using S3 Event notifications to trigger a Lambda function eliminates the need to continuously poll the S3 bucket for new files or involve additional services. The <code>s3:ObjectCreated</code> event ensures that the Lambda function is triggered immediately when a new file is uploaded. The Lambda function then invokes or initiates the transcoding service, leading to prompt video processing.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:&nbsp;<span><strong>Set up S3 Event Notifications to send a message to an AWS Lambda function whenever a new object is created in the bucket, and then have the Lambda function trigger the transcoding service.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says:<strong><span>Configure Amazon S3 to publish an event with the video's S3 path to an Amazon SQS queue when a new file is uploaded. Then, have the transcoding service poll the queue</span>&nbsp;</strong>is incorrect. While possible,&nbsp;this requires implementing additional polling logic within the transcoding service to continuously check the queue for new messages.&nbsp;With AWS Lambda, you can directly handle the S3 event without the need for an intermediary queue.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<strong><span>Use AWS Lambda to continuously poll the S3 bucket for new files and trigger the transcoding service when a new file is found</span>&nbsp;</strong>is incorrect because it’s inefficient. Continuously polling the S3 bucket would consume unnecessary resources and might not trigger the transcoding service as quickly as the <code>s3:ObjectCreated</code> event.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Use Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to stream the data from the S3 bucket to AWS Lambda, and then have Lambda trigger the transcoding service when a new file is detected</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because it’s overkill for this scenario. While Kinesis Data Streams can be used with S3 and Lambda for real-time data processing, it’s not necessary for this use case. The&nbsp;<code>s3:ObjectCreated</code> event can directly trigger a Lambda function, which is a straightforward and more efficient solution.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/EventNotifications.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/EventNotifications.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-s3.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-s3.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out these Amazon S3 and AWS Lambda Cheat Sheets:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":20,"sourceNumber":40,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A company manages a large amount of data stored in various AWS services. They need to create a data pipeline that extracts data from an Amazon RDS DB instance, transforms the data using AWS Glue jobs, and then loads the transformed data into an Amazon Redshift cluster. The data in the DB instance is updated daily at midnight, and the transformed data should be available in the Redshift cluster by 2 AM. The transformation job takes about an hour to complete.</p>\n<p>Which of the following solutions would best meet these requirements? (Select TWO).</p>","answers":["Create an AWS Glue connection for the Amazon RDS DB instance and the Amazon Redshift cluster.","Set up an AWS Glue ETL job to transform the data and an AWS Glue trigger to start the ETL job daily after the RDS instance is updated at midnight.","Set up an AWS Glue ETL job to transform the data and an AWS Glue trigger to start the ETL job every hour.","Create an AWS Glue connection for the Amazon RDS instance and the Amazon Redshift cluster, and then use Amazon EMR to transform the data.","Use AWS Glue to create a data lake that stores the raw data from the Amazon RDS instance. Query the data lake using Amazon Athena."],"correct":[0,1],"explanation":"<p>AWS Glue&nbsp;connection&nbsp;is a Data Catalog object that stores login credentials, URI strings, virtual private cloud (VPC) information, and more for a particular data store. AWS Glue crawlers, jobs, and development endpoints use connections in order to access certain types of data stores. You can use connections for both sources and targets and reuse the same connection across multiple crawler or extract, transform, and load (ETL) jobs.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/2324355da0b44ba6cf05f756525e4159b09165bd90a4ddf47a18e3f74c43668d.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>In AWS Glue, you can create Data Catalog objects called triggers, which you can use to either manually or automatically start one or more crawlers or extract, transform, and load (ETL) jobs. Using triggers, you can design a chain of dependent jobs and crawlers.&nbsp;When&nbsp;<em>fired</em>, a trigger can start specified jobs and crawlers. A trigger fires on demand, based on a schedule, or based on a combination of events.</p>\n<p>A trigger can exist in one of several states. A trigger is either&nbsp;<code>CREATED</code>,&nbsp;<code>ACTIVATED</code>, or&nbsp;<code>DEACTIVATED</code>. There are also transitional states, such as&nbsp;<code>ACTIVATING</code>. To temporarily stop a trigger from firing, you can deactivate it. You can then reactivate it later.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answers are:</p>\n<p><span><strong>- Create an AWS Glue connection for the Amazon RDS DB instance and the Amazon Redshift cluster.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span><strong>- Set up an AWS Glue ETL job to transform the data and an AWS Glue trigger to start the ETL job daily after the RDS instance is updated at midnight.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <strong><span>Set up an AWS Glue ETL job to transform the data and an AWS Glue trigger to start the ETL job every hour</span>&nbsp;</strong>is incorrect because it&nbsp;sets up the ETL job to run every hour. This doesn’t align with the requirement that the data in the RDS instance is updated daily at midnight. Running the ETL job every hour would result in unnecessary processing and could lead to inconsistencies if the ETL job runs while the RDS instance is being updated.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Create an AWS Glue connection for the Amazon RDS instance and the Amazon Redshift cluster, and then use Amazon EMR to transform the data</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because it&nbsp;uses Amazon EMR to transform the data. Amazon EMR is a cloud-native big data platform for processing large datasets, which might not be necessary for this use case. AWS Glue, on the other hand, is a fully managed ETL service that is more suitable for this use case. It can easily connect to the RDS instance and Redshift cluster, transform the data, and load the transformed data into the Redshift cluster.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use AWS Glue to create a data lake that stores the raw data from the Amazon RDS instance. Query the data lake using Amazon Athena</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. This option focuses more on ad-hoc querying of untransformed data rather than executing scheduled ETL jobs to transform and load data into Redshift.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-connections.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-connections.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/about-triggers.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/about-triggers.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":21,"sourceNumber":42,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A retail company uses an Amazon Redshift cluster to store daily sales data. Several tables within this cluster are updated daily with new sales records. The company wants to automate the monitoring process, which verifies whether the tables are successfully updated daily or if any updates are still pending. Additionally, it should be accurately reflected in an Amazon DynamoDB table.</p>\n<p>Which of the following options would be the most appropriate way to load the status of Redshift tables automatically? (SELECT TWO)</p>","answers":["Create a Lambda function that leverages Amazon Redshift API to query the status of each table’s data load in the Redshift cluster. Load the status recording in the Amazon DynamoDB table.","Configure an Amazon EventBridge rule to trigger the Lambda function at a specific time each day.","Enable Redshift's built-in integration with DynamoDB to automatically sync the table statuses without the need for additional AWS services or scripting.","Set up a direct connection between Redshift and DynamoDB using a VPC peering connection to sync table statuses automatically.","Utilize an EC2 instance to run a cron job that checks the Redshift table statuses and updates the DynamoDB table accordingly."],"correct":[0,1],"explanation":"<p>AWS Lambda is a serverless computing service that runs code in response to events and automatically manages the computing resources required by that code. It is ideal for executing small pieces of code (functions) in response to events. Amazon Redshift is a fully managed, petabyte-scale data warehouse service in the cloud. It allows users to run fast, complex queries against vast amounts of data. Amazon DynamoDB is a key-value and document database that delivers single-digit millisecond performance at any scale, making it a great choice for keeping real-time status updates. Lastly, Amazon EventBridge is a serverless event bus that makes it easy to connect applications together using data from your apps, integrated Software-as-a-Service (SaaS) applications, and AWS services.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/4c741ad819b076683e89bb2ff25b449a8ba17ef87939ba759aa35b7b1a455dbf.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>AWS Lambda can interact with Amazon Redshift to check the status of the tables and then update the Amazon DynamoDB table accordingly. This eliminates the need for managing servers or runtime environments, focusing solely on the code that monitors and updates the table statuses. Amazon EventBridge can be used to trigger this Lambda function at regular intervals, ensuring the process is automated and consistent.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answers are:</p>\n<p><span>- <strong>Create a Lambda function that leverages Amazon Redshift API to query the status of each table’s data load in the Redshift cluster. Load the status recording in the Amazon DynamoDB table.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span>- <strong>Configure an Amazon EventBridge to trigger the Lambda function at a specific time each day.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Enable Redshift's built-in integration with DynamoDB to automatically sync the table statuses without the need for additional AWS services or scripting </strong></span>is incorrect because there is no such built-in feature or direct integration that allows Amazon Redshift to automatically sync its table statuses with DynamoDB.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Set up a direct connection between Redshift and DynamoDB using a VPC peering connection to sync table statuses automatically</strong></span> is incorrect. While VPC peering can facilitate communication between two services within AWS, it does not inherently provide a method for data synchronization or the automatic updating of table statuses. The syncing of data would still require a custom implementation, such as a script or an application, to handle the logic and data transfer between Redshift and DynamoDB. Thus, simply setting up a VPC peering connection does not address the automation or the logic required to sync the table statuses.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Utilize an EC2 instance to run a cron job that checks the Redshift table statuses and updates the DynamoDB table accordingly</strong></span> is incorrect. While you can use EC2 instance to run a cron job, it introduces additional complexity and management overhead. An Amazon EC2 instance would need to be provisioned, configured, and maintained. The cron job would require a script to interact with both Redshift and DynamoDB, necessitating ongoing maintenance and monitoring to ensure its continuous operation.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/data-api.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/data-api.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/redshift-lambda-function-queries\">https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/redshift-lambda-function-queries</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Lambda Cheatsheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":22,"sourceNumber":43,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A company has a complex ETL pipeline that involves multiple AWS Glue jobs. Each job performs tasks such as data extraction, cleaning, and loading data into a Redshift data warehouse for analytics. The company wants to orchestrate these jobs into a single workflow with minimal operational overhead.</p>\n<p>Which AWS resource would be the most suitable for this use case?</p>","answers":["AWS Step Functions state machine","AWS Batch","AWS Glue workflows","Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA)"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p><strong>AWS Glue workflows</strong> provide a visual interface for orchestrating complex extract, transform, and load (ETL) activities involving multiple crawlers, jobs, and triggers. Each workflow manages the execution and monitoring of all its jobs and crawlers. As a workflow runs each component, it records execution progress and status. This provides you with an overview of the larger task and the details of each step.</p>\n<p>You can create a workflow from an AWS Glue blueprint or manually build a workflow a component at a time using the AWS Management Console or the AWS Glue API. Triggers within workflows can start both jobs and crawlers and can be fired when jobs or crawlers are complete. By using triggers, you can create large chains of interdependent jobs and crawlers.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/79d3261f4172b9278b920867a7dc6721294434a7f1d436ffa3249079da58ce54.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>In addition to triggers within a workflow that defines job and crawler dependencies, each workflow has a start trigger. There are three types of start triggers: Schedule, On-demand, and EventBridge event. With this trigger type, AWS Glue can be an event consumer in an event-driven architecture.</p>\n<p>This would require the least operational overhead as it is fully managed and designed for this exact use case.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:<strong><span> AWS Glue workflows.</span></strong></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>AWS Step Functions state machine&nbsp;</strong></span>is incorrect because this option takes more work. When a Glue job is initiated from a Step Function state machine, there isn't an immediate response indicating job completion. Instead, you'd have to implement custom logic within the state machine to continuously poll the status of a job. This is not the case with AWS Glue Workflow, where you can simply use the built-in conditions to manage running jobs in sequence or parallel.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>AWS Batch</strong></span> is incorrect because it is intended for running batch computing jobs. It is not designed for orchestrating ETL jobs or managing data workflows.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA)</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because, while Amazon MWAA provides a managed environment for running Apache Airflow workflows, it is not primarily designed for orchestrating Glue jobs. It is more general-purpose and may require more manual configuration and maintenance for ETL workflows compared to AWS Glue.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/simplify-data-pipelines-with-aws-glue-automatic-code-generation-and-workflows/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/simplify-data-pipelines-with-aws-glue-automatic-code-generation-and-workflows/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":23,"sourceNumber":44,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p><span>An e-commerce company aims to consolidate customer data from various sources into a data lake stored as CSV files on Amazon S3. A data engineer must ensure changes in the data sources are replicated in the data lake using Change Data Capture (CDC) operations. The solution must be designed cost-effectively and support ACID (Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability) properties to ensure data integrity.</span></p>\n<p><span>Which of the following options would meet the requirements?</span></p>","answers":["Implement ETL operations on the data in S3 using AWS Glue and then query the transformed data using Amazon Athena.","Leverage an open-source data lake format like Apache Hudi with an Amazon EMR cluster to capture and manage changed data.","Use the AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS) to replicate data changes to S3.","Leverage an open-source data lake format like Apache Iceberg with Amazon EMR Serverless to capture and manage changed data."],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p><span><strong>Amazon EMR Serverless</strong>&nbsp;is a deployment option for Amazon EMR that offers a serverless runtime environment. This feature simplifies the management of analytics applications that utilize modern open-source frameworks, such as Apache Spark and Apache Hive. With EMR Serverless, you don't need to configure, optimize, secure, or manage clusters to run applications using these frameworks.</span></p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/af483e5d9a2a172a35d9f93845c9fc823ac3b7c291cf06880c4ff426de468df3.png\" alt=\"Amazon EMR Serverless\"></p>\n<p><strong>Apache Iceberg</strong> is a distributed, community-driven data table format that is licensed under Apache 2.0 and is fully open-source. It simplifies data processing for large datasets stored in data lakes. Data engineers prefer Apache Iceberg because it is fast, efficient, and reliable at any scale, while also maintaining a record of how datasets change over time. Additionally, Apache Iceberg easily integrates with popular data processing frameworks, including Apache Spark, Apache Flink, Apache Hive, and Presto.</p>\n<p>A <strong>data lake</strong> is a centralized repository that enables you to store all your structured and unstructured data at any scale. A data transaction refers to a series of data exchanges conducted as a single operation. For instance, when a customer withdraws money from a bank account, the bank performs multiple data exchanges simultaneously in one data transaction. This includes verifying that the account has enough balance, confirming the customer's identity, and debiting the withdrawal amount from the account.</p>\n<p>A transactional data lake is a type of data lake that not only stores data at scale but also supports transactional operations. It ensures that the data is accurate and consistent and allows you to track changes to data and its structure over time. These characteristics are collectively known as Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, and Durability (ACID).</p>\n<p><strong>- Atomicity</strong>&nbsp;guarantees that each transaction is a single event that either succeeds or fails completely; there is no half-way status.</p>\n<p><strong>- Consistency</strong>&nbsp;ensures that all data written is valid according to the defined rules of the data lake, ensuring that data is accurate and reliable.</p>\n<p><strong>- Isolation</strong>&nbsp;ensures multiple transactions can occur at the same time without interfering with each other, ensuring&nbsp;that each transaction executes independently.</p>\n<p><strong>- Durability</strong>&nbsp;means that data is not lost or corrupted once a transaction is submitted. Data can be recovered in the event of a system failure, such as a power outage.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Leverage an open-source data lake format like Apache Iceberg with Amazon EMR Serverless to capture and manage changed data.&nbsp;</strong></span>Iceberg supports ACID transactions, scalable CDC operations, and schema evolution directly on Amazon S3. Combined with EMR Serverless, it offers a cost-effective, cloud-native architecture that eliminates the need to manage infrastructure while ensuring data integrity.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Implement ETL operations on the data in S3 using AWS Glue and then query the transformed data using Amazon Athena</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because, while it is a valid approach for data transformation and analysis, it might not be the most cost-effective for the given scenario. AWS Glue and Amazon Athena are powerful services, but they might just be unnecessary for simply capturing changes in the data (CDC).&nbsp;Additionally, they do not inherently support ACID properties.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Leverage an open-source data lake format like Apache Hudi with an Amazon EMR cluster to capture and manage changed data</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. Although Apache Hudi does support CDC and ACID semantics, using EMR can be more expensive and operationally complex compared to serverless or open-source alternatives.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use the AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS) to replicate data changes to S3</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. While AWS DMS can be used for CDC, it may not be as cost-effective for large-scale data lakes as using an open-source format like Apache Iceberg. The scenario specifically stated that the solution must be designed to be cost-effective. Additionally,&nbsp;it does not provide ACID properties for the data lake, which are crucial for ensuring data integrity.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/what-is/apache-iceberg/\">https://aws.amazon.com/what-is/apache-iceberg/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/EMR-Serverless-UserGuide/emr-serverless.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/EMR-Serverless-UserGuide/emr-serverless.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/EMR-Serverless-UserGuide/using-iceberg.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/EMR-Serverless-UserGuide/using-iceberg.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon S3 Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":24,"sourceNumber":46,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A data engineer is developing a Python-based data processing service. The service must be exposed as private REST APIs using Amazon API Gateway. The data engineer must ensure the service can handle a steady load with quick, low-latency responses.</p>\n<p>How can the service be deployed in the LEAST amount of effort?</p>","answers":["Deploy the service on AWS Lambda functions with SnapStart. Integrate the functions with API Gateway.","Create AWS Lambda functions with provisioned concurrency for the service and configure API Gateway to invoke the functions.","Deploy the service on Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (Amazon EKS). Configure API Gateway to route requests to an EKS cluster.","Implement the service for API Gateway on an Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS) cluster."],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>In Lambda,&nbsp;concurrency&nbsp;is the number of in-flight requests that your function is currently handling. There are two types of concurrency controls available:</p>\n<div>\n<p><strong>Reserved concurrency</strong> – This represents the maximum number of concurrent instances allocated to your function. When a function has reserved concurrency, no other function can use that concurrency. Configuring reserved concurrency for a function incurs no additional charges.</p>\n<p><strong>Provisioned concurrency</strong> – This is the number of pre-initialized execution environments allocated to your function. These execution environments are ready to respond immediately to incoming function requests. Configuring provisioned concurrency incurs additional charges to your AWS account.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/8ae0c2aa8360a2dd31fcfec2883be4e2797560c9ddb4e81b6db8e27728f54cff.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n</div>\n<p>When you invoke a Lambda function, the invocation is routed to an&nbsp;execution environment&nbsp;to process the request. When a function has not been used for some time, when you need to process more concurrent invocations, or when you update a function, new execution environments are created. The creation of an execution environment takes care of installing the function code and starting the runtime. Depending on the size of your deployment package, and the initialization time of the runtime and of your code, this can introduce latency for the invocations that are routed to a new execution environment. This latency is usually referred to as a “cold start”.&nbsp;For most applications, this additional latency is not a problem. For some applications, however, this latency may not be acceptable.</p>\n<p>When you enable Provisioned Concurrency for a function,&nbsp;the Lambda service&nbsp;will initialize the requested&nbsp;number of execution environments&nbsp;so they can be ready to respond to invocations.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:&nbsp;<span><strong>Create AWS Lambda functions with provisioned concurrency for the service and configure API Gateway to invoke the functions.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Deploy the service on AWS Lambda functions with SnapStart. Integrate the functions with API Gateway</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because SnapStart is designed to improve cold start performance primarily for functions that are not invoked frequently. From the given scenario, it requires high and steady load handling with quick execution, SnapStart may not provide the same level of readiness and performance consistency as provisioned concurrency, which is explicitly designed to keep functions warm and ready to execute.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <strong><span>Deploy the service on Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (Amazon EKS). Configure API Gateway to route requests to an EKS cluster</span></strong>&nbsp;is incorrect.&nbsp;Setting up an EKS cluster involves managing Kubernetes, which is complex and requires a significant amount of setup and operational management, even with AWS managing the Kubernetes infrastructure. This option would require much more effort compared to the serverless options.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <strong><span>Implement the service for API Gateway on an Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS) cluster</span></strong>&nbsp;is incorrect. While Amazon ECS is a fully managed container orchestration service, it still requires more setup and management effort compared to AWS Lambda. Thus, it's not the least effort compared to AWS Lambda.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/provisioned-concurrency.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/provisioned-concurrency.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/new-provisioned-concurrency-for-lambda-functions/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/new-provisioned-concurrency-for-lambda-functions/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":25,"sourceNumber":47,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A data engineering team is working on a project that uses Amazon Athena for ad-hoc querying and Amazon EMR for big data processing. They have set up a multi-step pipeline where raw data is processed by EMR, stored in an S3 bucket, and then queried using Athena. However, they are encountering delays in their pipeline due to high latencies in data processing with EMR. They have checked the configuration of the EMR cluster and found no issues.</p>\n<p>What could be the MOST likely reason behind this issue?</p>","answers":["The data queried by Athena is not in a columnar format.","The EMR cluster is located in a region different from Athena.","The S3 bucket where the data is stored is in a region different from the EMR cluster.","The Athena query execution limit has been reached."],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Amazon Web Services runs on servers in data centers around the world, which are grouped by geographical Region. When you launch an Amazon EMR cluster, you must specify a Region. You might choose a particular Region to reduce latency, minimize costs, or address regulatory requirements.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/9c7b2fd856d0cb7bac71dbad0d0f92ae72748585c390dac7533b2919193d1a05.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>For best performance, it’s recommended that the cluster be launched in the same region as your data. For instance, if the Amazon S3 bucket stores your input data in the US West (Oregon) Region, you should launch your cluster in the US West (Oregon) Region to avoid cross-region data transfer fees. If you use an Amazon S3 bucket to receive the output of the cluster, you would also want to create it in the US West (Oregon) Region.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:&nbsp;<span><strong>The S3 bucket where the data is stored is in a region different from the EMR cluster.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>The data queried by Athena is not in a columnar format&nbsp;</strong></span>is incorrect because the format of the data queried by Athena primarily affects the performance of Athena queries, not the latency in accessing EMR</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>The EMR cluster is located in a different region than Athena</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. The location of the Athena service itself does not directly impact the performance of EMR, which is responsible for processing the data before storing it in the S3 bucket. As long as the EMR cluster and the S3 bucket are in the same region, the location of Athena should not be a major factor contributing to the EMR processing delays.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>The Athena query execution limit has been reached</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because&nbsp;these limits are primarily related to Athena's query performance and would not directly impact the performance of EMR.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-plan-region.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-plan-region.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this&nbsp;Amazon EMR Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-emr/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-emr/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":26,"sourceNumber":49,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A healthcare company currently hosts its databases on Amazon EC2 Instances, which run the latest version of the Microsoft SQL Server database. The company wants to migrate its database to an Amazon RDS for Microsoft SQL Server.</p>\n<p>During the course of the migration process, the data analytics team needs to export large datasets, which entail complex SQL joins across multiple tables. The exported data should be stored in Amazon S3 in Apache Parquet columnar storage format.</p>\n<p>What should the Data Engineer use to fulfill the requirements in the MOST operationally efficient way?</p>","answers":["Create a view in the SQL Server on Amazon EC2 to consolidate the required data. Set up an AWS Glue job to pull data from this view, transform it into Parquet format, and store it in an S3 bucket. Schedule this job to run daily using AWS Glue triggers.","Set up a view in the Microsoft SQL Server database using a SQL Server Agent. Initiate an AWS Glue crawler to interpret the view, followed by an AWS Glue job to fetch and relocate the data in Parquet format to S3, scheduling the job for daily execution.","Implement an AWS Lambda function that employs Java Database Connectivity (JDBC) to query the EC2-hosted SQL Server databases, extract the essential data, transform it into Parquet format, and then transfer it to an S3 bucket. Employ Amazon EventBridge to schedule the Lambda function's daily execution.","Set up AWS Step Functions to orchestrate the data extraction process. Use Amazon Athena to perform the SQL joins and convert the data into Parquet format. Schedule the Step Functions workflow to run daily, and have Athena store the output directly in an S3 bucket."],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>AWS Glue is a fully managed extract, transform, and load (ETL) service that makes it easy for customers to prepare and load their data for analytics. You simply point AWS Glue to your data stored on AWS, and AWS Glue discovers your data and stores the associated metadata (e.g., table definition and schema) in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Once cataloged, your data is immediately searchable, queryable, and available for ETL. AWS Glue is serverless, so there's no infrastructure to set up or manage, allowing you to focus on your data rather than the resources needed to manage it.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/f54e4498ed4a25efac096f87e6ae6763c7199df76b74990dcce56dfe7928ce76.png\" alt=\"\">Using AWS Glue to extract data from a SQL Server database hosted on Amazon EC2, transform it into Apache Parquet format, and load it into Amazon S3 is an operationally efficient approach. This process leverages AWS Glue's capabilities to handle complex ETL jobs without the need for extensive infrastructure management. The transformation to Parquet format is particularly beneficial for analytics, as Parquet is optimized for query performance and cost efficiency in analytics workloads.</p>\n<p>Hence the correct answer is: <span><strong>Create a view in the SQL Server on Amazon EC2 to consolidate the required data. Set up an AWS Glue job to pull data from this view, transform it into Parquet format, and store it in an S3 bucket. Schedule this job to run daily using AWS Glue triggers.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Set up a view in the Microsoft SQL Server database using a SQL Server Agent. Initiate an AWS Glue crawler to interpret the view, followed by an AWS Glue job to fetch and relocate the data in Parquet format to S3, scheduling the job for daily execution</strong></span> is incorrect. While this option also employs AWS Glue, it introduces an additional step with the Glue crawler, which is designed to catalog data sources and infer schemas. This step could be redundant and add unnecessary complexity. Additionally, the crawling process might introduce delays and additional considerations for schema changes, which could affect the overall operational efficiency compared to the direct use of a Glue ETL job.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <strong><span>Implement an AWS Lambda function that employs Java Database Connectivity (JDBC) to query the EC2-hosted SQL Server databases, extract the essential data, transform it into Parquet format, and then transfer it to an S3 bucket. Employ Amazon EventBridge to schedule the Lambda function's daily execution</span>&nbsp;</strong>is incorrect. Implementing a custom solution with AWS Lambda primarily requires more development effort and introduces more points of potential failure. Lambda functions have time and resource limitations, which might not be ideal for processing large datasets. Additionally, managing the JDBC connections, handling data transformations manually, and ensuring the data is correctly written to S3 in Parquet format add layers of complexity.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Set up AWS Step Functions to orchestrate the data extraction process. Use Amazon Athena to perform the SQL joins and convert the data into Parquet format. Schedule the Step Functions workflow to run daily, and have Athena store the output directly in an S3 bucket</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. This would require copying the data to S3 first, which adds unnecessary complexity and steps. Athena is more suited for querying data already in S3, not for performing joins on data in an EC2-hosted SQL Server.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/what-is-glue.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/what-is-glue.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-job.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-job.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Glue Cheatsheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":27,"sourceNumber":50,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A company has a data pipeline that uses Lambda functions to handle complex logic, including executing Amazon Athena queries. These queries must be run in a specific order as part of the Lambda functions. The results of these queries are used in real-time decision-making, so it’s important that the pipeline can handle failures gracefully. The company wants to orchestrate this process in a way that allows for easy monitoring and error handling.</p>\n<p>Which of the following options would be the most efficient and reliable way to orchestrate this process? (SELECT TWO.)</p>","answers":["Use AWS Batch to manage the Athena queries.","<span>Utilize AWS Glue Python shell job with client <code>start_query_execution</code> API to run the Athena Queries.</span>","Employ Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA) to orchestrate the Athena queries.","<span>Implement AWS Lambda to run the Athena queries using the <code>start_query_execution</code> API.</span>","<span>Use AWS Step Functions workflow to orchestrate the Athena queries. Use the <code>get_query_execution</code> API to check the status of the queries. Implement a Wait state in the workflow to check if a query has been completed before running the next query.</span>"],"correct":[3,4],"explanation":"<p>AWS Lambda is a serverless compute service that lets you run your code without provisioning or managing servers. You can use AWS Lambda to run the Athena queries using the&nbsp;<code>start_query_execution</code>&nbsp;API. In a complex data pipeline where queries are dependent on each other and need to be run in a specific order, AWS Lambda’s event-driven nature is beneficial. It can be configured to trigger based on specific events, ensuring the correct sequence of query execution.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/66bf31c8ecbc9af2bce2fe9774ce340c5b80c795299060d0c0aee72dcee51750.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>AWS Step Functions is a serverless workflow service that lets you coordinate the components of distributed applications as a series of steps in a visual workflow. You can use AWS Step Functions to orchestrate the Athena queries and ensure they are run in the correct order. The&nbsp;<code>get_query_execution</code>&nbsp;API can be used to check the status of the queries. A wait state can be implemented in the Step Functions state machine to ensure that queries are run in the correct order.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answers are:</p>\n<p><span><strong>- Implement AWS Lambda to run the Athena queries using the&nbsp;<code>start_query_execution</code>&nbsp;API.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span><strong>- Use AWS Step Functions workflow to orchestrate the Athena queries. Use the&nbsp;<code>get_query_execution</code>&nbsp;API to check the status of the queries. Implement a wait state in the Step Functions state machine to ensure that queries are run in the correct order.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use AWS Batch to manage the Athena queries</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because AWS Batch is used for batch computing workloads, not for running Athena queries. It does not provide the same level of control over the execution order of the queries as AWS Lambda or AWS Step Functions.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Utilize AWS Glue Python shell job with client&nbsp;<code>start_query_execution</code>&nbsp;API to run the Athena Queries</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because while AWS Glue can be used to run Athena queries, it is primarily used for ETL jobs.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Employ Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA) to orchestrate the Athena queries</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because while Amazon MWAA can be used to orchestrate workflows, it is more complex to set up and manage compared to AWS Lambda or AWS Step Functions. It may not be the most efficient choice for this specific use case.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/welcome.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/welcome.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/welcome.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/welcome.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out these AWS Lambda and AWS Step Functions Cheat Sheets:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-step-functions/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-step-functions/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":28,"sourceNumber":52,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A data engineer uses Amazon Athena to run SQL queries against data stored in Amazon S3. They need a solution to enforce settings across all queries to ensure they use the same query results location in Amazon S3.</p>\n<p>Which approach can the team use to enforce these settings?</p>","answers":["Use Amazon S3 Bucket Policies","Use AWS IAM Roles","Use Service Control Policies (SCPs)","Use Amazon Athena Workgroups"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p><strong>Amazon Athena Workgroups</strong>&nbsp;allows you to separate your query execution across different teams, workloads, and applications or even run them in isolation from other queries. Workgroups provide a way to track query-related metrics, set limits to manage costs and enforce settings across all queries within the workgroup. Each workgroup has its own query history, metrics, data usage, and performance statistics, allowing for better resource allocation and cost management.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/6511228ea637ff9910557a7abbbc811844de2e9f87bb7aea9fb2dc43138dd995.gif\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>Workgroups also help you simplify the onboarding of new users to Athena. By selecting&nbsp;<strong>Override client-side settings</strong>, you enforce a predefined configuration on all queries within a workgroup. Users no longer have to configure a query results output location or S3 encryption keys. These settings default to the parameters defined for the workgroup where those queries execute. Additionally, each workgroup maintains a unique query history and saved query inventory, making queries easier for you to track down.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:&nbsp;<span><strong>Use Amazon Athena Workgroups.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Use Amazon S3 Bucket Policies</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because this primarily focuses on managing permissions on the S3 bucket itself. It does not provide a way to enforce settings across all Athena queries in a specific environment.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Use AWS IAM Roles</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because this only applies in managing access to AWS resources, not Athen queries.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Use Service Control Policies (SCPs)</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because SCPs are only designed to manage permissions in AWS Organizations and do not have the capability to enforce settings like query results location in Amazon S3.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/separating-queries-and-managing-costs-using-amazon-athena-workgroups/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/separating-queries-and-managing-costs-using-amazon-athena-workgroups/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/workgroups-procedure.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/workgroups-procedure.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Athena Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":29,"sourceNumber":53,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A multinational corporation is planning to migrate its on-premises PostgreSQL database to Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL. Since the company runs critical operations on the database, the migration must be completed with minimal downtime. The company also wants to ensure data consistency by validating the migrated data.</p>\n<p>Which AWS service would be the most suitable for this scenario?</p>","answers":["AWS Database Migration Service","AWS Data Transfer Terminal","AWS Transfer Family","AWS DataSync"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p><strong>AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS)</strong> is a web service you can use to migrate data from your database that is on-premises, on an Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS) DB instance, or in a database on an Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) instance to a database on an AWS service. These services can include a database on Amazon RDS or a database on an Amazon EC2 instance. You can also migrate a database from an AWS service to an on-premises database. You can migrate between source and target endpoints that use the same database engine, such as from an Oracle database to an Oracle database. You can also migrate between source and target endpoints that use different database engines, such as from an Oracle database to a PostgreSQL database.</p>\n<p><strong><img src=\"exams/assets/a93e0cfe80cbdb73df5be18cde5fe7cdb2380b9fe695b10958faf025247a3596.png\" alt=\"https://media.tutorialsdojo.com/public/AWS DMS-11JUN2026.png\"></strong></p>\n<p>You can use AWS DMS to migrate databases to the AWS Cloud quickly and securely. Your source database remains fully operational during the migration, which minimizes downtime for applications that rely on the database. You can reduce the migration time by using AWS DMS to create a task that captures ongoing changes after you complete an initial full-load migration through a process called&nbsp;change data capture (CDC).</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:&nbsp;<span><strong>AWS Database Migration Service.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span><strong>AWS Data Transfer Terminal</strong></span> is incorrect because this service is only intended for large-scale data transfers into Amazon S3, not for live database migrations or replication. It does not provide features for minimizing downtime or validating relational database consistency.</p>\n<p><span><strong>AWS Transfer Family</strong></span> is incorrect because Transfer Family typically provides managed support for file transfer protocols (FTP, SFTP, FTPS). It is useful for moving files into and out of AWS, but it is not suitable for migrating relational databases with replication and validation requirements.</p>\n<p><span><strong>AWS DataSync</strong>&nbsp;</span>is incorrect because it is merely a data transfer service. It can help automate and accelerate data movement&nbsp;between on-premises storage systems and AWS storage services. However, it does not support database migration.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/migrate-an-on-premises-oracle-database-to-amazon-rds-for-oracle.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/migrate-an-on-premises-oracle-database-to-amazon-rds-for-oracle.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/migrate-an-on-premises-oracle-database-to-amazon-rds-for-postgresql-by-using-an-oracle-bystander-and-aws-dms.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/migrate-an-on-premises-oracle-database-to-amazon-rds-for-postgresql-by-using-an-oracle-bystander-and-aws-dms.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Database Migration Service Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-database-migration-service/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-database-migration-service/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":30,"sourceNumber":54,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>An organization has recently launched a new product line globally. This has led to a significant increase in data queries on their Amazon Redshift cluster. The company’s data engineering team must adjust its Redshift infrastructure to manage concurrent queries effectively.</p>\n<p>Which step would meet the requirements?</p>","answers":["Recreate the Redshift cluster and activate concurrency scaling in the settings during creation.","Activate concurrency scaling at the workload management (WLM) queue level in the Redshift cluster.","Optimize the distribution keys and sort keys to improve query performance.","Enable result caching in Redshift to reuse query results."],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>The&nbsp;concurrency scaling&nbsp;feature in Amazon Redshift automatically adds and removes capacity by adding concurrency scaling to handle demands from thousands of concurrent users, thereby providing consistent SLAs for unpredictable and spiky workloads such as BI reports, dashboards, and other analytics workloads.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/14afa3011ee91dd2ce1f9cb018646ce3e67aeac41d0356f49a7ee42b3cfee256.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>Amazon Redshift workload management (WLM) enables users to flexibly manage priorities within workloads so that short, fast-running queries won't get stuck in queues behind long-running queries.</p>\n<p>Amazon Redshift WLM creates query queues at runtime according to&nbsp;<em>service classes</em>, which define the configuration parameters for various types of queues, including internal system queues and user-accessible queues. From a user perspective, a user-accessible service class and a queue are functionally equivalent. For consistency, this documentation uses the term&nbsp;<em>queue</em>&nbsp;to mean a user-accessible service class as well as a runtime queue.</p>\n<p>When you run a query, WLM assigns the query to a queue according to the user's user group or by matching a query group that is listed in the queue configuration with a query group label that the user sets at runtime.</p>\n<p>With manual WLM, Amazon Redshift configures one queue with a&nbsp;<em>concurrency level</em>&nbsp;of five, which enables up to five queries to run concurrently, plus one predefined Superuser queue with a concurrency level of one. You can define up to eight queues. Each queue can be configured with a maximum concurrency level of 50. The maximum total concurrency level for all user-defined queues (not including the Superuser queue) is 50.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Activate concurrency scaling at the workload management (WLM) queue level in the Redshift cluster.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Recreate the Redshift cluster and activate concurrency scaling in the settings during creation&nbsp;</strong><span>is incorrect.&nbsp;Recreating the Redshift cluster is a significantly disruptive process that involves downtime and potential data migration challenges. Concurrency scaling can be activated on an existing cluster without the need for recreation. </span></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Optimize the distribution keys and sort keys to improve query performance</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. This option would&nbsp;only help reduce the amount of data scanned and the overall execution time of an individual query. It won't affect the performance of running concurrent queries.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Enable result caching in Redshift to reuse query results</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. Result caching primarily benefits scenarios where identical or very similar queries are frequently executed. It won't help with the issue of scaling to manage a large number of unique, concurrent queries, which is the challenge described in the scenario.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_workload_mngmt_classification.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_workload_mngmt_classification.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/scale-read-and-write-workloads-with-amazon-redshift/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/scale-read-and-write-workloads-with-amazon-redshift/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/</a><strong>&nbsp;</strong></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":31,"sourceNumber":55,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A development team is currently managing numerous AWS Glue jobs through the AWS Glue Console. They aim to host the jobs’ source code in a repository to efficiently track code changes. Additionally, they require changes made in the repository to be automatically reflected in the Glue jobs.</p>\n<p>Which of the following will meet the requirements with the LEAST amount of development overhead?</p>","answers":["Use GitHub for version control and use the AWS Glue Studio’s version control feature for syncing changes.","Use GitHub for version control and AWS CodePipeline for continuous integration and delivery.","Use GitLab for version control and AWS CodeBuild for continuous integration and delivery.","Use Bitbucket and AWS CodePipeline for continuous integration and delivery."],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>AWS Glue is a serverless, scalable data integration service that simplifies the discovery, preparation, and combination of data from various sources for analytics, machine learning, and application development. It allows developers to version control their ETL scripts and workflows with its integration with Git, an open-source version control system.</p>\n<p>This integration works for all AWS Glue job types, both visual and code-based, and offers built-in integration with GitHub. It also facilitates the use of automation tools like Jenkins and AWS CodeDeploy to deploy AWS Glue jobs. AWS CodePipeline, on the other hand, is a fully managed continuous delivery service that helps automate the release process, enabling rapid release of new features.</p>\n<p>This combination meets all the requirements stated in the scenario, providing version control for ETL scripts and workflows, automating the detection of new code commits, running automated tests, and deploying successful builds to the AWS account.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/758469ad3fc1fe3d2e537a8a51f5f74bb10fa1114eb502cbff9b04bb1d0b5f90.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>If you have remote repositories and want to manage your AWS Glue jobs using your repositories, you can use AWS Glue Studio or the AWS CLI to sync changes to your repositories and your jobs in AWS Glue. When you sync changes this way, you're pushing the job from AWS Glue Studio to your repository or pulling from the repository to AWS Glue Studio.</p>\n<p>With Git integration in AWS Glue Studio, you can:</p>\n<div>\n<p>- Integrate with Git version control systems, such as GitHub, GitLab, and Bitbucket</p>\n<p>- Edit AWS Glue jobs in AWS Glue Studio, whether you use visual jobs or script jobs, and sync them to a repository</p>\n<p>- Parameterize sources and targets in jobs</p>\n<p>- Pull jobs from a repository and edit them in AWS Glue Studio</p>\n<p>- Test jobs by pulling from branches and/or pushing to branches utilizing multi-branch workflows in AWS Glue Studio</p>\n<p>- Download files from a repository and upload jobs into AWS Glue Studio for cross-account job creation</p>\n<p>- Use your automation tool of choice (for example, Jenkins, AWS CodeDeploy, etc.)</p>\n</div>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong> Use GitHub for version control and use the AWS Glue Studio's version control feature for syncing changes.</strong></span>&nbsp;Existing integration between Glue Studio and Git eliminates the need to manage multiple services or learn new tooling. This significantly reduces development time and effort.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong> Use GitHub for version control and AWS CodePipeline for continuous integration and delivery&nbsp;</strong></span>is incorrect. Although this option addresses the version control requirement with GitHub, it introduces additional layers of complexity. You'd typically need to configure AWS CodePipeline, adding more steps and potential points of failure. It's more overhead compared to directly using AWS Glue Studio's built-in feature.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use GitLab for version control and AWS CodeBuild for continuous integration and delivery</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. While this solution is possible, it would just require additional configuration to ensure changes made in the repository are automatically reflected in the AWS Glue jobs. This additional configuration might involve scripting or integration efforts to deploy changes to AWS Glue, leading to increased development overhead.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Use Bitbucket and AWS CodePipeline for continuous integration and delivery&nbsp;</strong></span>is incorrect. Similar to the other incorrect option, while this is valid, it may require additional configuration and overhead to deploy the changes made in the repository to AWS Glue.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/glue/features/\">https://aws.amazon.com/glue/features/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/code-versioning-using-aws-glue-studio-and-github/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/code-versioning-using-aws-glue-studio-and-github/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/edit-job-add-source-control-integration.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/edit-job-add-source-control-integration.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":32,"sourceNumber":58,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A data engineering team at a company is using Amazon Redshift for its data warehousing needs. There is a table with a column named <code>category</code>. The team needs to create a query that returns a list of data where the <code>category</code> field starts with either “TAD” or “AGO”.</p>\n<p>Which of the following Redshift SQL queries would correctly fulfill the team’s requirement?</p>","answers":["<span><code>SELECT * FROM data_table WHERE REGEXP_COUNT(category, '^TAD') OR REGEXP_COUNT(category, '^AGO');</code></span>","<span><code>SELECT * FROM table WHERE category = '^TAD|GO';</code></span>","<span><code>SELECT * FROM data_table WHERE REGEXP_INSTR(category, '^TAD') OR REGEXP_INSTR(category, '^AGO');</code></span>","<span><code>SELECT * FROM table WHERE category IN ('TAD%', 'AGO%');</code></span>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p><strong><code>REGEXP_COUNT&nbsp;</code></strong>function searches a string for a regular expression pattern and returns an integer that indicates the number of times the specified pattern occurs in the string. If no match is found, then the function returns 0.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/8e9e78cd09903bffe5a0580d9898234baf4e33907104629a47a50c8cd322b245.png\" alt=\"Amazon Redshift - REGEXP_COUNT function\"><br>\nThe command above uses the&nbsp;<strong><code>REGEXP_COUNT&nbsp;</code></strong>function, which counts the number of matches of the given regular expression pattern in the category field. The pattern <strong><code>^TAD</code></strong> ensures that the match happens at the start of the string, and similarly, <strong><code>^AGO</code></strong> checks for matches at the start of the string with \"AGO\".</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong><code>SELECT * FROM data_table WHERE REGEXP_COUNT(category, '^TAD') OR REGEXP_COUNT(category, '^AGO');</code></strong></span></p>\n<p>The command below is incorrect because the = operator is primarily used for exact string matching, and <strong><code>'^TAD|GO'</code></strong> is being treated as a literal string. The pattern <strong><code>'^TAD|GO'</code></strong> would match exact categories that are exactly equal to ^TAD or GO, which is not the intended behavior. This does not apply regular expression logic as expected for matching categories starting with \"TAD\" or \"AGO\".</p>\n<p><span><strong><code></code></strong></span></p>\n<p><span><strong><code>SELECT * FROM table WHERE category = '^TAD|GO';<br>\n</code></strong></span></p>\n<p>The command below is incorrect because <strong><code>REGEXP_INSTR</code></strong> typically returns the position of the first match of the regular expression in the string, rather than counting matches. In this case, checking the position is not the most efficient or straightforward way to filter rows where the category starts with \"TAD\" or \"AGO\". Using <strong><code>REGEXP_COUNT</code></strong> would be more suitable because it directly counts matches and avoids checking the position.</p>\n<p><span><strong><code>SELECT * FROM data_table WHERE REGEXP_INSTR(category, '^TAD') OR REGEXP_INSTR(category, '^AGO');<br>\n</code></strong></span></p>\n<p>The command below is incorrect because the IN operator is used for exact matches, not for pattern matching. While the % symbol is used for wildcard matching, the IN operator does not interpret the % as a pattern but as a literal string. This makes the query incorrect, as IN cannot be used with patterns like TAD% and AGO%.</p>\n<p><span><strong><code>SELECT * FROM table WHERE category IN ('TAD%', 'AGO%');</code></strong></span></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/REGEXP_COUNT.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/REGEXP_COUNT.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/doc-history.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/doc-history.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":33,"sourceNumber":59,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A data engineer at a large e-commerce company is tasked with improving the performance of ad-hoc queries run on customer behavior data stored in Amazon S3 using Amazon Athena. The data is partitioned by date, and over time, the number of partitions has grown significantly, leading to an increase in Athena query planning time.</p>\n<p>Which of the following should the data engineer implement to reduce the Athena query planning time?</p>","answers":["Utilize AWS Glue Data Catalog partition indexes and Athena partition projection based on the S3 bucket prefix.","Optimize query performance by refining data partitioning and using bucketing strategies in Amazon S3","Make use of the Amazon EMR S3DistCP to consolidate smaller files into larger ones and reorganize data by more efficient partitioning schemes.","Improve data formats by converting to columnar formats like Apache Parquet."],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>AWS Glue partition indexes are an important configuration to reduce overall data transfers and processing, and reduce query processing time. In the AWS Glue Data Catalog, the GetPartitions API is used to fetch the partitions in the table. The API returns partitions that match the expression provided in the request. If no partition indexes are present on the table, all the partitions of the table are loaded and then filtered using the query expression provided by the user in the <code>GetPartitions </code>request. The query takes more time to run as the number of partitions increases on a table with no indexes. With an index, the <code>GetPartitions </code>request tries to fetch a subset of the partitions instead of loading all the partitions in the table.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/c5c3b65ca826849941cd4da742f07347012a8a92b62ad518591a1cb2df276f48.jpg\"></p>\n<p>You can use partition projection in Athena to speed up query processing of highly partitioned tables and automate partition management. In partition projection, Athena calculates partition values and locations using the table properties that you configure directly on your table in AWS Glue. The table properties allow Athena to 'project' or determine the necessary partition information instead of having to do a more time-consuming metadata lookup in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Because in-memory operations are often faster than remote operations, partition projection can reduce the runtime of queries against highly partitioned tables. Depending on the specific characteristics of the query and underlying data, partition projection can significantly reduce query runtime for queries that are constrained on partition metadata retrieval.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Utilize AWS Glue Data Catalog partition indexes and Athena partition projection based on the S3 bucket prefix.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says:<span><strong> Optimize query performance by refining data partitioning and using bucketing strategies in Amazon S3</strong></span> is incorrect. While refining data partitioning and using bucketing strategies can optimize query performance, it does not address the issue of Athena query planning time related to managing a large number of partitions. This approach may still result in a large number of partitions that need to be managed and queried, which can adversely affect Athena query planning time.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Make use of the Amazon EMR S3DistCP to consolidate smaller files into larger ones and reorganize data by more efficient partitioning schemes</strong> </span>is incorrect. S3DistCP is only a tool that can be used to combine files and minimize the number of objects that Athena scans. This can result in faster query execution times. However, it does not directly reduce the amount of time Athena takes to manage partition metadata during query planning. Its primary benefit is data organization and file size optimization, rather than direct improvement of Athena's partition management.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Improve data formats by converting to columnar formats like Apache Parquet</strong></span> is incorrect. Converting data to a columnar format can improve query performance, but it does not address the issue of longer query planning time in Athena due to a large number of partitions. While columnar storage enables faster retrieval of columns, it doesn't directly impact the management or retrieval of partition metadata, which causes longer planning time in Athena.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/glue-best-practices.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/glue-best-practices.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partition-projection.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partition-projection.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/improve-query-performance-using-aws-glue-partition-indexes/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/improve-query-performance-using-aws-glue-partition-indexes/</a></p>\n<p><strong>Check out these AWS Glue and Amazon Athena Cheat Sheets:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":34,"sourceNumber":63,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A tech startup is currently utilizing Amazon Athena for their data analytics operations, using SQL queries for ETL tasks via the Create Table As Select (CTAS) feature. They have recently decided to transition to Apache Spark due to its capabilities in handling large datasets across distributed clusters.</p>\n<p>Which feature of Amazon Athena would allow the startup to use Apache Spark for their data analytics operations?</p>","answers":["Athena Data Catalog","Athena data source","Athena query settings","Athena workgroup"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>An Athena workgroup is a feature of Amazon Athena that allows for the grouping of queries. By default, each account has a primary workgroup, and the default permissions allow all authenticated users access to this workgroup. Each workgroup that you create shows saved queries and query history only for queries that ran in it. This separates your queries from other queries within an account and makes it more efficient for you to locate your own saved queries and queries in history.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/8517c43c90657d6e15da76b79e804afc0fef68ada1d6f4dacd83883fd9950766.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>When a workgroup is enabled with Apache Spark, it allows the use of Apache Spark with Amazon Athena for data analytics operations. This is particularly useful for organizations that need to process large datasets across distributed clusters, as it allows them to leverage the advanced data processing capabilities of Apache Spark within the Amazon Athena environment. You can set up workgroup-wide settings and enforce their usage by all queries that run in a workgroup. The settings include query results location in Amazon S3, expected bucket owner, encryption, and control of objects written to the query results bucket. When you enforce workgroup-wide settings, all queries run in this workgroup use workgroup settings. This happens even if their client-side settings may differ from workgroup settings.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:<strong><span> Athena workgroup.</span></strong></p>\n<p><span><strong>Athena Data Catalog</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because Athena Data Catalog is just a central location where you can store and retrieve table metadata across all your data stored in Amazon S3. It does not provide the capability to use Apache Spark with Amazon Athena.</p>\n<p><span><strong>Athena data source</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because Athena data sources refer to the databases and tables that Athena uses to run queries. While they are an important part of using Athena, they do not enable the use of Apache Spark with Amazon Athena.</p>\n<p><span><strong>Athena query settings</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because Athena query settings allow you to manage query-related configurations in Amazon Athena. They do not enable the use of Apache Spark with Amazon Athena.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/user-created-workgroups.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/user-created-workgroups.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/notebooks-spark-getting-started.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/notebooks-spark-getting-started.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Athena Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":35,"sourceNumber":64,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A company is preparing to deploy an Amazon EMR cluster, utilizing Apache Spark for data extraction, transformation, and loading (data ETL). The cluster must sustain the company's performance under enduring workloads, and high reliability is of the utmost importance. The cluster must store data persistently.</p>\n<p>Which storage and computing will satisfy the requirements MOST cost-effectively?</p>","answers":["Utilize Elastic Map Reduce File System (EMRFS) as the storage and Graviton instances for computing.","Utilize the local file system as the storage and spot instances for computing.","Utilize Hadoop Distributed File System (HDFS) as the storage and x86 instances for computing.","Utilize Amazon Elastic File System (EFS) as the storage and spot instances for computing."],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p><strong>Amazon EMR</strong> (previously called Amazon Elastic MapReduce) is a managed cluster platform that simplifies running big data frameworks, such as Apache Hadoop and Apache Spark, on AWS to process and analyze vast amounts of data. Using these frameworks and related open-source projects, you can process data for analytics purposes and business intelligence workloads.</p>\n<p>Based on the EMR Workflow, planning the storage is an essential first step in configuring Amazon EMR.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/bf21d4551b6e6167bb214b0d0b774e6b28c954dbca1ae5f7b4dcda8b9abc004e.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>You can choose from a variety of file systems to store input data, output data, and log files:</p>\n<p><strong>Hadoop Distributed File System (HDFS)</strong> - HDFS is a distributed, scalable, and portable file system for Hadoop. HDFS distributes the data it stores across instances in the cluster, storing multiple copies of data on different instances to ensure that no data is lost if an individual instance fails. One advantage is that it's fast; a disadvantage is that it's ephemeral storage which is reclaimed when the cluster ends. It's best used for caching the results produced by intermediate job-flow steps.</p>\n<p><strong>EMR File System (EMRFS)</strong> - EMRFS is an implementation of the Hadoop file system used for reading and writing regular files from Amazon EMR directly to Amazon S3. EMRFS provides the convenience of storing persistent data in Amazon S3 for use with Hadoop.</p>\n<p><strong>Local file system</strong> - The local file system refers to a locally connected disk. When you create a Hadoop cluster, each node is created from an Amazon EC2 instance that comes with a preconfigured block of pre-attached disk storage called an instance store. Data on instance store volumes persists only during the lifecycle of its Amazon EC2 instance.</p>\n<p>For computing,<strong> AWS Graviton</strong> processors are custom-designed by AWS to enable the best price performance for workloads in Amazon EC2. Amazon EC2 instances powered by AWS Graviton2 processors provide up to 40% better price performance over comparable fifth-generation x86-based instances for a wide variety of workloads.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Utilize Elastic Map Reduce File System (EMRFS) as the storage and Graviton instances for computing.</strong></span> EMRFS is the most suitable file system for storing persistent data. At the same time, Graviton instances are the best choice in terms of cost-effectiveness while not sacrificing performance.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Utilize Hadoop Distributed File System (HDFS) as the storage and x86 instances for computing</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. While HDFS is fast and can perform better, it is unsuitable for storing persistent data. HDFS uses temporary storage, available only during the cluster's life cycle. x86 instances are inferior to Graviton as Amazon EC2 instances powered by AWS Graviton2 processors provide up to 40% better price performance over comparable fifth-generation x86-based instances for various workloads.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Utilize the local file system as the storage and spot instances for computing</strong></span> is incorrect because of unreliability. Data on a local file system only persists at the lifecycle of the EC2 instance and will be deleted after EC2 instance termination. The company will also run enduring workloads that make spot instances unsuitable because spot instances can be interrupted when EC2 reclaims the instance to repurpose capacity. While this option can be cheaper, there are better options than this one, considering high reliability and performance.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Utilize Amazon Elastic File System (EFS) as the storage and spot instances for computing</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. While EFS is suitable for many use cases, it is generally more expensive than S3 for data-intensive applications like Spark on EMR. Also, the use of spot instances, although cost-effective, could pose a risk of interruption, which may affect the reliability of the system.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/ec2/graviton/getting-started/\">https://aws.amazon.com/ec2/graviton/getting-started/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-plan-file-systems.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-plan-file-systems.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-gs.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-gs.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon EMR Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-emr/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-emr/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 3 · Câu bổ sung"}]};